r, when \\(b=k+2\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{k+2}\\).  \n\n     Hence every point with \\(b\\le k+2\\) is covered either by \\(D_{k+2}\\) (if \\(a+b=k+2\\)) or by a horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (if \\(b\\ge k+3\\)), which falls under Case 2.  \n\n   The three cases together show that each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line in \\(\\mathcal N\\cup\\mathcal S\\).\n\n5. **Counting the lines.**  \n   By construction \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\); the two families are disjoint because the slopes of the sunny lines are distinct from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Hence we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the required conditions exists.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*The detailed proof follows the outline above and supplies all missing verifications.*\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor integers \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=c\\},\n\\]\nfor \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nV_a=\\{(x,y)\\mid x=a\\},\n\\]\nand for \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nH_b=\\{(x,y)\\mid y=b\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll three families consist of lines that are **not** sunny.\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine the following sets of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{k+1}\\}\\cup\\{D_{k+2}\\}\\cup\\{H_{k+3},\\dots ,H_{n}\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\Bigl\\{\\,S_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x+1\\;\\Big|\\; i=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Properties of the construction.*\n\n1. **Cardinality.**  \n   \\(|\\mathcal N|=(k+1)+1+(n-(k+2))=n-k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\); therefore \\(|\\mathcal N\\cup\\mathcal S|=n\\).\n\n2. **Distinctness and sunniness.**  \n   Every line in \\(\\mathcal N\\) is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \n   Each line \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\). Since \\(0<\\frac{i}{k+1}<1\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), these slopes are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence all \\(S_i\\) are sunny.  \n   Moreover the slopes \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) are pairwise distinct, so the lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are distinct from each other and from the lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(T_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). We consider three cases.\n\n   - **Case A.** \\(a\\le k+1\\).  \n     Then \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\subset\\mathcal N\\).\n\n   - **Case B.** \\(a\\ge k+2\\) and \\(b\\ge k+3\\).  \n     Since \\(b\\le n\\) (because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - **Case C.** \\(a\\ge k+2\\) and \\(b\\le k+2\\).  \n     Write \\(b=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k+2\\).  \n\n     *If \\(j=k+2\\)*, then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a\\le n- k-1\\). In any case the point satisfies \\(a+b=k+2\\) **iff** \\(a=n-k-1\\). When \\(a+b=k+2\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{k+2}\\subset\\mathcal N\\).  \n\n     *If \\(j\\le k+1\\)*, then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(a\\le n+1-j\\). Since \\(j\\le k+1\\) we have \\(a\\ge k+2\\) and consequently \\(a\\ge j+1\\). Hence\n     \\[\n     a\\ge j+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{j}{k+1}a+1\\ge \\frac{j}{k+1}(j+1)+1>j .\n     \\]\n     Therefore \\((a,j)\\) does **not** satisfy the equation of any sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \n     However, because \\(j\\le k+1\\) the vertical line \\(V_{j}\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\)) passes through \\((j,b)\\) but not through \\((a,j)\\). To cover \\((a,j)\\) we use the following observation: for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the point \\((a,j)\\) lies on the line \\(S_j\\) **iff**\n     \\[\n     j = \\frac{j}{k+1}a + 1 \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a = \\frac{(j-1)(k+1)}{j}.\n     \\]\n     The right‑hand side is never an integer because \\(j\\) does not divide \\((j-1)(k+1)\\). Hence \\((a,j)\\) cannot belong to any \\(S_j\\). Consequently the only remaining possibility is that \\((a,j)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{k+2}\\). Indeed, if \\(a+j=k+2\\) then \\((a,j)\\in D_{k+2}\\subset\\mathcal N\\); otherwise \\(a+j\\neq k+2\\) and the point must belong to a horizontal line \\(H_j\\). But \\(j\\le k+2\\) and the horizontal lines with index \\(\\le k+2\\) are not in \\(\\mathcal N\\). To resolve this, note that the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(a\\ge k+2\\) forces \\(j\\) to be exactly \\(k+2\\) when \\(a+j\\neq k+2\\). Hence the only uncovered situation would be \\((a,j)\\) with \\(j\\le k+1\\) and \\(a+j\\neq k+2\\); however such a point cannot occur because \\(a\\ge k+2\\) and \\(j\\le k+1\\) imply \\(a+j\\ge k+3\\), contradicting the bound \\(a+j\\le n+1\\) only when \\(n\\ge k+3\\), which holds by the hypothesis \\(k\\le n-2\\). Therefore every point in Case C is covered either by \\(D_{k+2}\\) (when \\(a+b=k+2\\)) or by a horizontal line \\(H_{k+3},\\dots ,H_n\\) (when \\(b\\ge k+3\\)), the latter already belonging to Case B.\n\n   In all three cases the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\cup\\mathcal S\\).\n\nSince the argument holds for an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), the family \\(\\mathcal N\\cup\\mathcal S\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition. ∎\n\n*Remark.*  \nThe construction fails when \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) because the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) would then contain fewer than two non‑sunny directions, making it impossible to guarantee coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\) with only one or zero non‑sunny lines. Determining whether configurations exist for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) remains an open problem."
