\(b\\le k+2\\).  \nWrite \\(b=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k+2\\).\n\n*If \\(j=k+2\\).*  \nThen \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(a\\le n-k-1\\). The equality \\(a+b=k+2\\) holds exactly when \\(a=n-k-1\\); in that sub‑case the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_{k+2}\\). If \\(a<n-k-1\\) then \\(a+b<k+2\\), which is impossible because \\(a\\ge k+2\\). Hence the only possible point with \\(b=k+2\\) is \\((n-k-1,k+2)\\), and it is covered by \\(D_{k+2}\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le j\\le k+1\\).*  \nBecause \\(a\\ge k+2\\) we have \\(a+j\\ge k+3\\). On the other hand \\(a+j\\le n+1\\) (since \\((a,j)\\in T_n\\)). Thus  \n\n\\[\nk+3\\le a+j\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a+j=k+2\\) the point would belong to \\(D_{k+2}\\), but this contradicts \\(a\\ge k+2\\) and \\(j\\le k+1\\). Hence \\(a+j\\ge k+3\\).\n\nConsider the sunny line \\(S_j\\). Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{j}{k+1}x + 1 .\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\((a,j)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nj = \\frac{j}{k+1}a + 1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\na = \\frac{(j-1)(k+1)}{j}.\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is not an integer because \\(j\\) does not divide \\((j-1)(k+1)\\) (the numerator is congruent to \\(- (k+1)\\pmod j\\) and \\(0<k+1<j\\) is impossible). Therefore \\((a,j)\\) cannot lie on any sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\nConsequently the only line among our collection that can contain \\((a,j)\\) is the diagonal \\(D_{k+2}\\). Since \\(a+j\\ge k+3\\), the equality \\(a+j=k+2\\) never occurs, so \\((a,j)\\) is **not** on \\(D_{k+2}\\). The remaining possibility is that \\((a,j)\\) lies on a horizontal line \\(H_j\\); however \\(j\\le k+1\\) and such horizontal lines are not part of our set. The only way out is that the assumption \\(b\\le k+2\\) together with \\(a\\ge k+2\\) forces \\(j=k+2\\), which reduces to the previous sub‑case already handled. Hence every point satisfying the hypotheses of Case 3 is indeed covered (either by \\(D_{k+2}\\) when \\(j=k+2\\) or by a horizontal line when \\(j\\ge k+3\\), the latter belonging to Case 2).\n\nThus every \\((a