ee\nlines, all non\u2011vertical.\n\nConsider the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\).  For each such column we have\n\\(|C_a|\\ge 2\\).  Since a non\u2011vertical line can cover at most one point of a\ngiven column, covering all points of the \\(n-2\\) columns would require at\nleast \\(2(n-2)\\) non\u2011vertical lines.  But \\(|\\mathcal S|\\le n\\) (the whole\nfamily has only \\(n\\) lines), and for \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-2) > n .\n\\]\n\nThus it is impossible to cover all columns without using the corresponding\nvertical lines.  Consequently any admissible family must contain the vertical\nlines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\\); there are exactly \\(n-2\\) of them and they are\nnon\u2011sunny.  Only the remaining two lines (to reach the total of \\(n\\)) may be\nnon\u2011vertical, so the number of sunny lines is at most two. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\); Lemma\u202f2 gives a\nconfiguration with \\(k=2\\); Lemma\u202f3 shows that \\(k\\ge 3\\) is impossible.\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"

