in \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEvery line is either vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)) or non‑vertical.  A non‑vertical\nline meets a fixed column \\(\\{a\\}\\times\\mathbb R\\) in at most one point, because\nall points of the column have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  Hence, to cover the\n\\(c\\) points of a column without using the vertical line \\(x=a\\) we need at least\n\\(c\\) non‑vertical lines.  Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the total\nnumber of non‑vertical lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Sunny lines are a subset of\nthe non‑vertical lines, so \\(k\\le n\\).  The lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) is trivial. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are distinct and non‑sunny.  They cover all points of the\ncolumns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\).  The number of points already covered is\n\n\\[\nS_{n-k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{n-k}(n+1-i)\n       = (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points in \\(T_n\\) equals \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); therefore the\nstill uncovered points are\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-S_{n-k}= \\frac{k(2n-k+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y-1 = -\\frac12\\bigl(x-(n-j)\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_j\\) have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\); consequently each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\nA direct substitution shows that \\(L_j\\) passes through the points  \n\n\\[\n(n-j,1),\\;(n-j-2,2),\\;(n-j-4,3)\n\\]\n\nwhenever the coordinates are positive.  Because the abscissae \\(n-j\\) run\nthrough the set \\(\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the collection\n\\(\\{L_0,\\dots 