 with the point \\((2,2)\\) counted twice we obtain precisely \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}=2(n-2)\\) incidences, i.e. every point of \\(T'_n\\) is hit by one of the sunny lines.*  \n   *Thus the family  \n     \\[\n     \\{\\;x=1,\\;x+y=n+1,\\;\\ell_{3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\;\\}\n     \\]\n     contains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k=n-2\\) of which are sunny, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).*  \n\n   *To obtain smaller values of \\(k\\) we replace sunny lines by additional non\u2011sunny lines.  \n     Replacing a sunny line \\(\\ell_{j+2}\\) by a vertical line \\(x=j+2\\) (or a horizontal line \\(y=j+2\\)) covers the same two points of \\(T'_n\\) that \\(\\ell_{j+2}\\) covered, because those points lie on the column (or row) \\(x=j+2\\) (or \\(y=j+2\\)). Each such replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the total of \\(n\\) lines and the covering property. Repeating this operation \\(n-2-k\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).*\n\nHence the upper bound from Lemma\u202f1 is attainable for every admissible \\(k\\), completing the solution.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) at least two lines are not sunny; consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not horizontal we have \\(b_1,b_2,b_3\\) distinct; because it is not vertical we have \\(a_1,a_2,a_3\\) distinct; because it is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we have \\(a_1+b_1,\\;a_2+b_2,\\;a_3+b_3\\) distinct.  \n\nAll six numbers lie in the interval \\([1,n+1]\\).  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least two of them must coincide, a contradiction.  Hence **any sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points**.\n\nTherefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2},\n\\]\n\nthe remaining  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2k\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n* A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\) points.\n* A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points.\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nThus each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf only one non\u2011sunny line were used, it could cover at most \\(n\\) points, leaving at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2k-n>0\n\\]\n\npoints uncovered (the inequality holds for every admissible \\(k\\)).  Hence at least **two** non\u2011sunny lines are required, i.e. \\(n-k\\ge 2\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n#### 1.  Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_1\\) contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);  \n\\(\\ell_2\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nBoth lines are non\u2011sunny (vertical and slope \\(-1\\), respectively).\n\nAfter removing the points already covered by \\(\\ell_1\\cup\\ell_2\\) we are left with  \n\n\\[\nT'_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T'_n\\) is the triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\) shifted one unit to the right; it contains  \n\n\\[\n|T'_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nlattice points.\n\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=n-2\\) (maximal number of sunny lines)\n\nDefine, for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\),\n\n\\[\nm_j=j+1,\\qquad \n\\ell_{j+2}:\\; y=m_j(x-2)+2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_{j+2}\\) passes through the point \\((2,2)\\) and through the point \\((j+2,j+2)\\).  \nThe slopes \\(m_j\\) are distinct and satisfy \\(m_j\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); therefore all \\(\\ell_{j+2}\\) are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\nThe set of points \\(\\{(2,2),(3,3),\\dots ,(n,n)\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) points.  \nBecause each line \\(\\ell_{j+2}\\) contains exactly two points of \\(T'_n\\) (namely \\((2,2)\\) and \\((j+2,j+2)\\)), the total number of incidences of sunny lines with points of \\(T'_n\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-2)=|T'_n|\n\\]\n\nby (1).  Hence every point of \\(T'_n\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\).  \n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(k=n-2\\) are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.  Obtaining smaller values of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStart from the configuration for \\(k=n-2\\) described above.  \nIf we replace a sunny line \\(\\ell_{j+2}\\) by a vertical line \\(x=j+2\\) (or, equivalently, by a horizontal line \\(y=j+2\\)), the two points of \\(T'_n\\) that were covered by \\(\\ell_{j+2}\\) (namely \\((2,2)\\) and \\((j+2,j+2)\\)) are still covered, because both lie on the column \\(x=j+2\\) (or on the row \\(y=j+2\\)).  \n\nEach such replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the total number of lines (still \\(n\\)) and the covering property.  Performing exactly \\((n-2)-k\\) replacements yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n\\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and Lemma\u202f2 provides explicit constructions attaining every number of sunny lines from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).  Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
