ruction for a given \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).*  \n\n   - Keep the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell _{\\,t}\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\{-\\,k+1,\\,-\\,k+2,\\dots ,0,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     (If \\(k=0\\) this set is empty.)  \n     These \\(k\\) lines are distinct and sunny.\n\n   - For every integer \\(b\\) with \\(3\\le b\\le n\\) keep the **horizontal** line  \n\n     \\[\n     h_b:\\; y=b .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such horizontals.\n\n   - Finally add the two horizontal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     h_1:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad h_2:\\; y=2 .\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;+\\;(n-2)\\;+\\;2\\;=\\;n .\n   \\]\n\n   The number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\) (the horizontals are not sunny).\n\n4. **Verification that the construction covers all required points.**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(b\\ge 3\\).*  \n   By definition we kept the horizontal line \\(h_b\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(h_b\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(b=2\\).*  \n   If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(t=b-a=2-a\\) satisfies \\(-k+1\\le t\\le k-1\\); consequently \\((a,2)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell _t\\) from (3).  \n   If \\(a>k\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) (because \\(a+2\\le n+1\\)), and the horizontal line \\(h_2\\) contains \\((a,2)\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(b=1\\).*  \n   If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(t=b-a=1-a\\) also belongs to the index set \\(\\{-k+1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); hence \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell _t\\).  \n   If \\(a>k\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) (since \\(a+1\\le n+1\\)), and the horizontal line \\(h_1\\) contains \\((a,1)\\).\n\n   In every case the point \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the constructed lines.  \n   Hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   The total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\); therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n   The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \n   Consequently no other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}:=\\{\\ell _t\\mid t\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ -\\,n+2\\le t\\le n-2\\},\n\\qquad \n\\ell _t:\\; y = x + t .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell _t\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is *sunny* (it is not parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThe lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Coverage by the family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\))\n\nFor every integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) there exists\n\\(t\\) with \\(-\\,n+2\\le t\\le n-2\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in\\ell _t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPut \\(t:=b-a\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-b\\le a-n-1\\le -b+2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\,n+2\\le b-a\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\) lies in the index range of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).  \nSubstituting \\(t=b-a\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell _t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = x + (b-a)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b = a + (b-a),\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for the point \\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell _t\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Construction for a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{S}_k:=\\{\\ell _t\\mid t\\in T_k\\},\n   \\qquad \n   T_k:=\\{-\\,k+1,\\,-\\,k+2,\\dots ,0,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(k=0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal{S}_k\\) is empty.)  \n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal{S}_k\\) are sunny and distinct.\n\n2. **Horizontal lines for rows \\(b\\ge 3\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(3\\le b\\le n\\) keep the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   h_b:\\; y=b .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such lines.\n\n3. **Horizontal lines for rows \\(b=1,2\\).**  \n   Add the two horizontal