set  \n\n\\[\nT_k\\subset I,\\qquad |T_k|=k .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_k:=\\{\\ell_t\\mid t\\in T_k\\}\\quad\\text{(sunny diagonals)},\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_k:=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{x=a\\mid a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\},\\;a-1\\notin T_k\\}\n\\quad\\text{(vertical lines)},\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_k:=\\{y=b\\mid b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;b-1\\notin T_k\\}\n\\quad\\text{(horizontal lines)}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n*Counting.*  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) contains \\(k\\) lines.  \nExactly \\(k\\) values of \\(a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) satisfy \\(a-1\\in T_k\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal V_k\\) contains \\(1+(n-1-k)=n-k\\) vertical lines.  \nSimilarly \\(\\mathcal H_k\\) contains \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines.  \nRemoving the redundant horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) leaves a total of  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)+(n-k)-1 = n\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\mathcal S_k\\cup\\mathcal V_k\\cup\\mathcal H_k\n\\]\n\nhas exactly \\(n\\) members, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification of the covering condition\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=1\\in\\mathcal V_k\\) contains the point.\n\n*If \\(a\\neq1\\)*, consider the integer \\(b-1\\).\n\n*   If \\(b-1\\in T_k\\), then \\(\\ell_{\\,b-1}\\in\\mathcal S_k\\).  \n    Its equation is \\(y=x+(b-1)\\); substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=b\\).  \n    Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on that sunny line.\n\n*   If \\(b-1\\notin T_k\\), then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_k\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every required lattice point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sunny‑line count\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) have slope 1 and are therefore sunny; the lines in \\(\\mathcal V_k\\) and \\(\\mathcal H_k\\) are vertical or horizontal and are not sunny.  Consequently the number of sunny lines in the configuration is exactly \\(|\\mathcal S_k|=k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nSince we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
