fy \\(b-a=n-1\\) and \\(b-a=-(n-1)\\) respectively; they are **not** covered by the diagonals in (2) and will be handled separately by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n4. **Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**\n\n   *Choose sunny diagonals.*  \n   Pick an arbitrary subset  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k\\subset I,\\qquad |T_k|=k .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_k:=\\{\\;\\ell_t : y=x+t\\mid t\\in T_k\\;\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) are sunny and distinct.\n\n   *Choose non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   C_k:=\\{\\,a\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid a-1\\notin T_k\\,\\},\n   \\qquad\n   R_k:=\\{\\,b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid b-1\\notin T_k\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (Thus \\(|C_k|=|R_k|=n-k\\).)\n\n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V_k:=\\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=a\\mid a\\in C_k\\,\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_k:=\\{\\,y=b\\mid b\\in R_k\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal V_k\\cup\\mathcal H_k\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal S_k|=k,\\qquad |\\mathcal V_k|=1+|C_k|=1+(n-k)=n-k+1,\\qquad |\\mathcal H_k|=|R_k|=n-k .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(y=1\\) would belong to \\(\\mathcal H_k\\) only when \\(0\\notin T_k\\); however it is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  Hence we **omit** \\(y=1\\) from \\(\\mathcal H_k\\) when it occurs.  This removes exactly one line, and the total number of distinct lines becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k+1)+(n-k)-1 = n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(a=1\\).*  \n   The vertical line \\(x=1\\in\\mathcal V_k\\) contains \\((1,b)\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(a\\neq1\\) and \\(a-1\\in T_k\\).*  \n   Then the diagonal \\(\\ell_{\\,a-1}\\in\\mathcal S_k\\) has equation \\(y=x+(a-1)\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=2a-1\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2a-1\\); moreover \\(b\\ge a\\) (otherwise \\(b-a\\) would be negative and not equal to \\(a-1\\)).  Hence the only possible value of \\(b\\) that satisfies both the diagonal equation and the inequality is \\(b=2a-1\\), which indeed lies in the required set.  Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on that sunny diagonal.\n\n   *Case 3: \\(a\\neq1\\) and \\(a-1\\notin T_k\\).*  \n   By definition \\(a\\in C_k\\), so the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V_k\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Case 4: \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\neq1\\).*  \n   Here \\(b-1=0\\).  If \\(0\\in T_k\\) then the diagonal \\(\\ell_{0}\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) and passes through \\((a,1)\\) only when \\(a=1\\), which is excluded; therefore \\(0\\notin T_k\\) and consequently \\(1\\in R_k\\).  The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) would have been in \\(\\mathcal H_k\\), but it is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\); thus \\((a,1)\\) is still covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from Case 3.\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\cup\\mathcal V_k\\cup\\mathcal H_k\\).  Hence the covering requirement is satisfied.\n\n6. **Sunny‑line count.**  \n   By construction \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while all lines in \\(\\mathcal V_k\\cup\\mathcal H_k\\) are horizontal or vertical and therefore non‑sunny.  Consequently the total number of sunny lines in the configuration is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n7. **Exhaustiveness.**  \n   Because we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  The construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Therefore no other non‑negative integer can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an