\\ell_{R}\\). A sunny line cannot contain either of these points (its slope is different from \\(-1\\)), therefore each of the two points requires a distinct non‑sunny line. Hence at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\). This rules out \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\nCombining the existence construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the impossibility of larger \\(k\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThus the only possible non‑sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{horizontal: }y=c,\\\\\n&\\text{vertical: }x=c,\\\\\n&\\text{diagonal of slope }-1:\\;x+y=c .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nThe points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) belong to the triangle \\(T_n\\).  \nBoth lie on distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{L}:x+y=n+1,\\qquad \\ell_{R}:x+y=2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it cannot contain either \\((1,n)\\) or \\((n,1)\\).  \nHence any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_{L}\\) and \\(\\ell_{R}\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The base set of non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH&=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 2\\le j\\le h\\,\\},\\\\[2pt]\nV&=\\{\\,x=j\\mid 2\\le j\\le v\\,\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad\nh=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil-1,\\;\\;\nv=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\n|H|=h,\\qquad |V|=v,\\qquad h+v=n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the two diagonal lines (5) we obtain a collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_0=\\{\\ell_{L},\\ell_{R}\\}\\cup H\\cup V\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nof exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines to be inserted\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ns_t:\\; y-x = t-1\\qquad(\\text{slope }+1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(s_t\\) are distinct and none is parallel to the forbidden directions, so they are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Replacement of non‑sunny lines\n\nWe delete from \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) exactly \\(k\\) lines, one for each sunny line added, according to the following deterministic rule:\n\n*Delete \\(\\ell_{R}\\) (the line \\(x+y=2\\)) if \\(k\\ge 1\\).  \nThen, for \\(j=2,3,\\dots\\) delete the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) when the next deletion is needed, and delete the vertical line \\(x=j\\) on the subsequent step, alternating between horizontal and vertical. Continue until a total of \\(k\\) deletions have been performed.*\n\nBecause \\(|H|+|V|=n-2\\ge k\\) (recall \\(k\\le n-2\\)), this procedure is always possible, and the lines removed are all distinct. Denote the resulting set of non‑sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{ns}}\\); then  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{ns}}| = n-k .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFinally set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{ns}}\\;\\cup\\;\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Three mutually exclusive situations are considered.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{L}\\). Since \\(\\ell_{L}\\) is never deleted, the point is covered.\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.** \\(a+b=2\\).  \nThe only point with this property is \\((1,1)\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell_{R}\\) has not been deleted, the point lies on \\(\\ell_{R}\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell_{R}\\) has been deleted (which happens exactly when \\(k\\ge 1\\)), then \\(b-a=0\\) and \\((1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_1\\) (because \\(s_1\\) is defined by \\(y-x=0\\)). Hence the point is covered in either sub‑case.\n\n---\n\n**Case 3.** \\(2<a+b<n+1\\).  \nDefine the difference \\(d=b-a\\).  \nObserve that \\(d\\) is an integer satisfying \\(- (n-2)\\le d\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Subcase 3a.* \\(d\\ge 0\\).  \nIf the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{ns}}\\) then the point is covered.  \nIf \\(y=b\\) has been deleted, then by the deletion rule the deletion occurred at the step when a horizontal line with index \\(b\\) was removed; this can happen only after the deletion of \\(\\ell_{R}\\), i.e. only when \\(k\\ge 1\\). In that situation the sunny line \\(s_{d+1}\\) (recall \\(d+1\\ge 1\\)) is present in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) and satisfies \\(y-x=d\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(s_{d+1}\\).\n\n*Subcase 3b.* \\(d<0\\).  \nIf the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{ns}}\\) the point is covered.  \nIf \\(x=a\\) has been deleted, the deletion must have occurred at the step when a vertical line with index \\(a\\) was removed; this can happen only after the deletion of the preceding horizontal line, hence only when \\(k\\ge 2\\). In this case the sunny line \\(s_{d+1}\\) (now \\(d+1\\le 0\\)) is present, and because \\(y-x=d\\) the point lies on it.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of larger values of \\(k\\)\n\nAs proved in (6), any admissible family must contain the two distinct non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_{L}\\) and \\(\\ell_{R}\\). Consequently at least two lines are non‑sunny, i.e. the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\). Hence configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessity (\\(k\\le n-2\\)) and the sufficiency (explicit constructions for each \\(k\\) in that range) have been established."
