on is complete.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-3\\) and the constructive proof that every\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) can be realized together give the exact\n   set of possible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\text{ are possible, and no other }k\\text{ works}.}\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must cover \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\)\nare *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions\n\\(\\ell_h:y=\\text{const}\\), \\(\\ell_v:x=\\text{const}\\), \\(\\ell_d:x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine the three “border’’ subsets of \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_n &=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nV_n &=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nD_n &=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2\\}=\\{(1,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach of these sets lies on a line of a forbidden direction:\n\\(H_n\\subseteq \\ell_h\\;(y=1)\\), \\(V_n\\subseteq \\ell_v\\;(x=1)\\), \\(D_n\\subseteq\n\\ell_d\\;(x+y=2)\\).\n\n*Claim.* A sunny line meets each of \\(H_n,V_n,D_n\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* If a line contains two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) then those points\nhave the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so the line is horizontal, i.e. parallel to\n\\(\\ell_h\\), contradicting sunniness. The same argument works for \\(V_n\\) and\n\\(D_n\\). ∎\n\nConsequently a sunny line can cover at most three points of the union\n\\(H_n\\cup V_n\\cup D_n\\). The total number of points in this union is\n\n\\[\n|H_n|+|V_n|+|D_n|-2 = n+n+1-2 = 2n-1,\n\\]\n\n(the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to all three sets, so we subtract it twice).\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most \\(3n\\) points of the\nunion, but \\(2n-1>3n\\) is false for \\(n