her cover a large “border’’ of \\(S_n\\):  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n     \\ell_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n     \\ell_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     These three lines are pairwise distinct and cover  \n\n     \\[\n     |\\ell_1\\cap S_n|+|\\ell_2\\cap S_n|+|\\ell_3\\cap S_n|-2\n     = (n)+(n-1)+(n-1)-2 = 3n-4\n     \\]\n\n     points (the two intersections \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) are counted twice and are subtracted).  \n   * After removing the points already covered, the remaining set  \n\n     \\[\n     R_n:=S_n\\setminus(\\ell_1\\cup\\ell_2\\cup\\ell_3)\n     \\]\n\n     has  \n\n     \\[\n     |R_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}-(3n-4)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\n     \\]\n\n     points.  \n   * The points of \\(R_n\\) lie strictly inside the triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).  \n     They can be paired so that each pair consists of two points that are **not** on a line parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \n     A convenient pairing is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\bigl\\{(i,j),\\;(j,i)\\bigr\\},\\qquad 2\\le i<j\\le n-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     For each such pair the line through the two points has slope \\(\\frac{j-i}{i-j}=-1\\) only when \\(i+j=n+1\\); but the equality \\(i+j=n+1\\) never occurs because both \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) are at most \\(n-1\\). Hence every line determined by a pair is **sunny**.  \n   * There are exactly \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\) points in \\(R_n\\), i.e. \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{4}\\) pairs.  \n     Since \\((n-2)(n-3)\\) is always even, the number of pairs is an integer.  \n     Denote by \\(\\mathcal S\\) the set of the corresponding sunny lines; we have  \n\n     \\[\n     |\\mathcal S|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{4}.\n     \\]\n\n   * At this stage we have used three non‑sunny lines and \\(|\\mathcal S|\\) sunny lines, totalling  \n\n     \\[\n     3+|\\mathcal S| = 3+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{4}=n-\\frac{(n-2)}{4}\n     \\]\n\n     lines.  To reach exactly \\(n\\) lines we add the necessary number of **extra non‑sunny lines** (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the diagonal) that do not introduce any new points of \\(S_n\\); for instance we may add copies of the line \\(x=0\\) (which contains no point of \\(S_n\\)).  Adding at most one such dummy line suffices because  \n\n     \\[\n     n- \\bigl(3+|\\mathcal S|\\bigr)=\\frac{n-2}{4}\\in\\{0,1\\}\n     \\]\n\n     for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Thus we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely  \n\n     \\[\n     k=|\\mathcal S| = \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{4}\n     \\]\n\n     sunny lines.  By deleting any subset of the sunny lines (and, if necessary, adding the same number of dummy non‑sunny lines) we can realise **any** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n     \\[\n     0\\le k\\le |\\mathcal S| = n-2 .\n     \\]\n\n   * Consequently for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit construction yields the complete classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
