any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least three non‑sunny\nlines, and consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A full covering using exactly \\(n\\) lines\n\nDefine the “interior’’ set\n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(H_n\\cup V_n\\cup D_n\\bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal\n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n\\(d_c\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\)) and contains exactly the points of\n\\(I_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=c\\).  The values of \\(c\\) that actually\noccur for points of \\(I_n\\) are \\(4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\nObserve that the three lines (7) together with the diagonals\n\n\\[\nd_c\\qquad (c=4,5,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nform a set of  \n\n\\[\n3+(n-3)=n\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of these lines:\n\n* points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) or \\(a+b=2\\) lie on the three lines (7);\n* a point \\((a,b)\\in I_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n  hence lies on the diagonal \\(d_c\\) in (11).\n\nThus the family (7)∪(11) covers all required points and contains **no sunny\nlines**; it realises the case \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Introducing sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\nChoose arbitrarily a subset \\(\\mathcal{C}\\subset\\{4,5,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with\n\\(|\\mathcal{C}|=k\\).  For each \\(c\\in\\mathcal{C}\\) replace the diagonal \\(d_c\\)\nby a line of a sunny direction that still passes through the same set of points.\nFor example, fix a slope \\(\\sigma\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (say\n\\(\\sigma=2\\)).  For each \\(c\\in\\mathcal{C}\\) pick any point \\((a,b)\\) on\n\\(d_c\\) and draw the line\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\; y-b=\\sigma