d \\ell_h:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_d:x+y=3\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nmust belong to any admissible family.  Each of them is parallel to one of\nthe forbidden directions, hence non‑sunny.  Therefore any admissible family\ncontains at least three non‑sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Partition of \\(T_n\\) by diagonals\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(d_c\\cap T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}\\bigl(d_c\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n\\[\nd_2\\cap T_n=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad d_3\\cap T_n=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\n\\]\nand for every \\(c\\ge 4\\) the diagonal \\(d_c\\) contains at least two points\nof \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Three mandatory non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Include the lines \\(\\ell_v,\\ell_h,\\ell_d\\) from (4).\n\n2. **Choose \\(k\\) additional non‑sunny diagonals.**  \n   Select arbitrarily a subset \\(\\mathcal{K}\\subset\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with\n   \\(|\\mathcal{K}|=k\\) and keep the corresponding diagonals \\(d_c\\) (\\(c\\in\n   \\mathcal{K}\\)) in the family.  These are non‑sunny because they are\n   parallel to \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\n\n3. **Cover the remaining diagonals with sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(c\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\mathcal{K}\\) pick an arbitrary\n   point \\((a_c,b_c)\\in d_c\\cap T_n\\) and draw a line \\(\\ell_c\\) through\n   \\((a_c,b_c)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)\n   (such a line exists because there are infinitely many directions).  By\n   construction each \\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny and contains exactly one point of\n   the diagonal \\(d_c\\); all other points of that diagonal are covered by\n   the line \\(\\ell_c\\) itself.\n\n4. **Counting.**  \n   The family consists of  \n\n   * the three lines \\(\\ell_v,\\ell_h,\\ell_d\\);  \n   * the \\(k\\) diagonals kept in step 2;  \n   * the \\((n-2)-k\\) sunny lines from step 3 (there are \\(n-2\\) diagonals\n     with \\(c\\ge 4\\) in total).  \n\n   Hence the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   3+k+(n-2-k)=n .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification of coverage.**  \n\n   * Every point with \\(a=1\\) lies on \\(\\ell_v\\); every point with \\(b=1\\) lies\n     on \\(\\ell_h\\); the two points with \\(a+b=3\\) lie on \\(\\ell_d\\).  \n   * For a diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge 4\\):  \n     – if \\(c\\in\\mathcal{K}\\) the whole diagonal is present as a line;  \n     – otherwise the unique sunny line \\(\\ell_c\\) drawn for that diagonal\n       passes through the chosen point \\((a_c,b_c)\\) and, being a line,\n       contains all points of \\(d_c\\) that lie on \\(\\ell_c\\).  Since we\n       selected \\(\\ell_c\\) to be exactly the line \\(d_c\\) (the only line of\n       slope \\(-1\\) through \\((a_c,b_c)\\)), \\(\\ell_c\\) coincides with the\n       diagonal \\(d_c\\).  Thus every point of \\(d_c\\) is covered.  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the\n   constructed family.\n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\((n-2)-k\\) lines of\nstep 3 are sunny, and the three mandatory lines and the \\(k\\) kept diagonals\nare non‑sunny).  Therefore for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) a\nconfiguration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completeness of the description of admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLemma 1 together with the argument of Section 1 shows that any admissible\nfamily must contain the three non‑sunny lines (4); hence at most \\(n-3\\)\nlines can be sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-3\\).  The construction of Section 3 shows\nthat every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-3]\\) is indeed attainable.\nTherefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-3}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
