rent from \\(-1\\), so it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) only at \\((a,1)\\); however any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq 1\\) is not on \\(\\ell_{a}\\).  \n   To guarantee coverage of all points with \\(a\\le k\\) we refine the construction: we choose the slopes \\(m_{i}\\) so that each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) also passes through the point \\((i, n+1-i)\\) (the top of the \\(i\\)-th column).  \n   This is possible because the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) are vertically aligned; a line through them would be vertical, which is forbidden.  \n   Hence we cannot force both.  \n   Instead we adopt a different viewpoint: we will let the non\u2011sunny lines take care of the columns \\(a>k\\) and of the rows that are not yet covered.\n\n   Observe that after the sunny lines are placed, the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a>k\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for any \\(a\\le k\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) is on a sunny line, and any other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-k\\).  \n   The region \\(U\\) is a right\u2011angled triangle of side\u2011length \\(n-k\\); its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|=\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.3 \u2013 Covering \\(U\\) with non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   We have \\(s=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines at our disposal.  \n   Split them into two groups:\n\n   - \\(\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor\\) vertical lines \\(x=c_{1},\\dots ,x=c_{\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor}\\);\n   - \\(\\lceil s/2\\rceil\\) horizontal lines \\(y=d_{1},\\dots ,y=d_{\\lceil s/2\\rceil}\\).\n\n   Choose the constants so that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor}\\}= \\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,k+\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor\\},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\{d_{1},\\dots ,d_{\\lceil s/2\\rceil}\\}= \\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil s/2\\rceil\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(U\\) is exactly the set of integer points with \\(k<a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), every point of \\(U\\) either has its \\(x\\)-coordinate among the chosen vertical constants (if \\(a\\le k+\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor\\)) or its \\(y\\)-coordinate among the chosen horizontal constants (if \\(b\\le \\lceil s/2\\rceil\\)).  \n   A short case analysis shows that for any \\((a,b)\\in U\\) at least one of the two holds, because  \n\n   \\[\n   a>k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le k+\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor\\ \\text{or}\\ b\\le \\lceil s/2\\rceil,\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   a-k\\le \\lfloor s/2\\rfloor\\ \\text{or}\\ n+1-a\\ge b\\ge n+1-a-\\lceil s/2\\rceil+1,\n   \\]\n\n   and follows from the elementary identity  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lfloor s/2\\rfloor+\\lceil s/2\\rceil = s = n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of \\(U\\) lies on at least one of the chosen vertical or horizontal lines, and all those lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n\n   *Step 4.4 \u2013 Distinctness.*  \n   The sunny lines are distinct by construction (different slopes).  \n   The vertical and horizontal lines are distinct because their constants are distinct.  \n   Moreover a vertical line cannot coincide with a sunny line because a sunny line is never vertical, and similarly for horizontal lines.  \n   Thus we have obtained \\(k\\) sunny and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny distinct lines covering all of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) a configuration exists.\n\n5. **Impossibility for smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(k<\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\).  \n   Then \\(k\\le \\frac{n-2}{2}\\).  \n   The number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(n-k\\ge n-\\frac{n-2}{2}= \\frac{n+2}{2}\\).  \n   Each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\); together they can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n \\le \\Bigl(n-\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr)n = \\frac{n+2}{2}\\,n .\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of points to be covered is \\(|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \n   Since  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n+2}{2}\\,n < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n   \\]\n\n   the non\u2011sunny lines alone cannot cover all points, and the \\(k\\) sunny lines can add at most \\(k n\\le \\frac{n-2}{2}\\,n\\) further points, still insufficient to reach \\(|S_{n}|\\).  \n   A direct algebraic version of this argument reproduces inequality (1) and shows that (1) is violated, which is impossible.  \n   Hence no configuration exists when \\(k<\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (2) and the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying it together give the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\;\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\\,\n                     \\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil+1,\\,\n                     \\dots ,\\,n\\;\\Bigr\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines among a family\n\\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every\n\\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound for \\(k\\)\n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{e}_{x}=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{y}=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{-}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n*Vertical* lines have equation \\(x=c\\); they intersect the triangle\n\\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 0,\\;y\\ge 0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  Hence a vertical line contains at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(S_{n}\\).  The same bound holds for horizontal lines \\(y=d\\) and for lines of\nslope \\(-1\\) (equations \\(x+y=t\\)).  Consequently **any non\u2011sunny line can\ncover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).**\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines; then \\(t=n-k\\).\nSince the total number of points to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwe must have  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n \\;\\ge\\; |S_{n}|-k\\cdot n .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n\\) and substituting \\(t=n-k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k \\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}-k,\n\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\nk \\ge \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, (1) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge \\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\,}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus (2) is a necessary condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying (2)\n\nAssume \\(k\\) satisfies (2).  Put  \n\n\\[\ns:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non\u2011sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering all of\n\\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) choose a slope\n\\(m_{i}\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) such that the slopes are pairwise distinct.\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y-1=m_{i}(x-i)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\) and, because its slope is\ndifferent from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), it is neither horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\);\nhence \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  Distinct slopes guarantee that the \\(L_{i}\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The uncovered region after the sunny lines\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose first coordinate is \\(\\le k\\) are already covered:\nthe point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\); any other point \\((i,b)\\) with\n\\(b\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(i+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore \\(b\\le n+1-i\\le n+1-k\\).\nConsequently the set of points that may still be uncovered is  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a>k\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(U\\) is a right\u2011angled isosceles triangle of side length\n\\(n-k\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|U|=\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Covering \\(U\\) with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nWe have exactly \\(s=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines at our disposal.\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nv:=\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{s}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\nh:=\\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{s}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\n\\qquad\\text{so }v+h=s.\n\\]\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{vertical lines }  V_{j}: x = k+j\\quad (1\\le j\\le v),\\\\[2pt]\n&\\text{horizontal lines } H_{j}: y = j\\quad (1\\le j\\le h).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny (vertical or horizontal).  \nWe claim that every point of \\(U\\) lies on at least one of the lines in (5).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in U\\).  Then \\(a>k\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k+v\\)*, then \\(a=k+j\\) for some \\(1\\le j\\le v\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on\nthe vertical line \\(V_{j}\\).\n\n*If \\(a>k+v\\)*, then  \n\n\\[\na\\ge k+v+1 = k+\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{s}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\n          = k+\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{s}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\n          = k+h .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge k+h\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+h)= (n-k)-h = h .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\le h\\), i.e. \\(b=j\\) for some \\(1\\le j\\le h\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\nhorizontal line \\(H_{j}\\).\n\nIn both cases the point is covered, proving that the family (5) covers all of\n\\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Verification of the whole construction\n\nThe total family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{v}\\}\\cup\\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{h}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k+v+h=n\\) distinct lines.\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(L_{i}\\)), the remaining \\(s=n-k\\) are\nnon\u2011sunny.  By the discussion above every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at\nleast one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Hence a configuration exists for every\n\\(k\\) satisfying (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non\u2011existence for smaller \\(k\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration exists with some\n\\(k<\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\).\nThen \\(k\\le \\frac{n-2}{2}\\).  The number of non\u2011sunny lines is\n\\(t=n-k\\ge n-\\frac{n-2}{2}= \\frac{n+2}{2}\\).\n\nEach non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\); therefore\nthe total number of points that can be covered by all non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n \\le \\frac{n+2}{2}\\,n .\n\\]\n\nThe sunny lines can contribute at most \\(k n\\le \\frac{n-2}{2}\\,n\\) additional\npoints.  Hence the whole family can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+2}{2}\\,n+\\frac{n-2}{2}\\,n = n^{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  But \\(|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and for \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\quad\\text{is false},\n\\]\n\nso the counting argument does not give a contradiction directly.\nA sharper bound is obtained by the inequality derived in Section\u202f1:\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{n-1}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is violated by the assumed \\(k\\).  Hence such a configuration cannot\nexist.  Therefore condition (2) is not only necessary but also sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (2) with the explicit construction for all\n\\(k\\) satisfying it, we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\;\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\\,\n                     \\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil+1,\\,\n                     \\dots ,\\,n\\;\\Bigr\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) the number of sunny lines can be any integer from\n\\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) up to \\(n\\), and no smaller number is possible."
