"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved the following necessary condition on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le n-2\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, at most \\(n-2\\) of them can be sunny.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Covering requirement as a hypergraph problem.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \n   Each line of the configuration contributes a subset of \\(S\\); the covering condition means that the family of these subsets forms a cover of \\(S\\).\n\n2. **Classification of lines.**  \n   A line is *non\u2011sunny* if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}: y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{2}: x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{3}: x+y=0 .\n   \\]\n   Hence a non\u2011sunny line has equation either \\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).\n\n3. **Counting points contributed by a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   For any integer \\(c\\) the line \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) meets the lattice points of \\(S\\) in at most two points, because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces the integer solutions of\n   \\[\n   b = ma + d,\\qquad d\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\]\n   to be confined to a segment of length \\(<2\\) in the \\(a\\)-direction. Consequently **every sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S\\).**\n\n4. **Counting points contributed by a non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) can contain many points of \\(S\\).  \n   In fact:\n   * \\(x=c\\) contains all points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\) \u2013 i.e. \\(n+1-c\\) points.\n   * \\(y=c\\) contains all points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\) \u2013 also \\(n+1-c\\) points.\n   * \\(x+y=c\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\) \u2013 i.e. \\(c-1\\) points (provided \\(c\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n   Hence a non\u2011sunny line can cover arbitrarily many points of \\(S\\).\n\n5. **Deriving the bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Since the total number of points to be covered is \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), we obtain the inequality\n   \\[\n   2k + \\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k} \\bigl|L_i\\cap S\\bigr|\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   where \\(L_i\\) denotes the \\(i\\)-th non\u2011sunny line.  \n   Each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points (the maximum occurs for the lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\)). Therefore\n   \\[\n   2k + (n-k)\\,n \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Simplifying,\n   \\[\n   2k + n^{2}-kn \\ge \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   k\\bigl(2-n\\bigr) \\ge -\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(2-n<0\\); dividing by this negative number reverses the inequality:\n   \\[\n   k \\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\ell_{i}\\cap S\\bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}: y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}: x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}: x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a non\u2011sunny line has an equation of one of the forms 