            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *column*  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\},\\qquad |C_a|=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|C_1|=n,\\;|C_2|=n-1,\\dots ,|C_n|=1,\n\\qquad\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nConsequently the only non‑sunny directions are vertical, horizontal and slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1\nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains exactly the points of the column \\(C_a\\);  \na horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points of the row  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\},\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b ;\n\\]\n\na line of slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\)) contains exactly the points of the anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |D_c|=c-1\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Directly from the definitions of the three families of points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2\nIf a line is sunny then it meets each column, each row and each anti‑diagonal in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct points of the same column have equal \\(x\\)-coordinates, so the line through them would be vertical, contradicting sunniness.  The same reasoning applies to rows (horizontal) and anti‑diagonals (slope \\(-1\\)). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Structure of a feasible family\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and let exactly \\(t\\) of them be sunny.  \nBecause a horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) would miss all points of the first column \\(C_1\\), the only useful non‑sunny lines are vertical.  Hence the remaining \\(n-t\\) lines are distinct vertical lines \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_{\\,n-t}\\) with \\(1\\le a_i\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### How many points can the vertical lines cover?\n\nThe size of column \\(C_a\\) is \\(n+1-a\\), which decreases as \\(a\\) grows.  \nTo maximise the number of covered points we therefore choose the columns with the smallest indices, i.e.  \n\n\\[\na_1=1,\\;a_2=2,\\dots ,a_{\\,n-t}=n-t .\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points covered by these vertical lines is\n\n\\[\nV(t)=\\sum_{a=1}^{\\,n-t}(n+1-a)\n      =\\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Points that remain uncovered\n\nThe columns not used are \\(C_{n-t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\); their sizes are \\(t,t-1,\\dots ,1\\).  Hence the number of points still uncovered after the vertical lines is\n\n\\[\nU(t)=\\sum_{i=1}^{t} i=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese uncovered points form the set\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_t=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le t,\\;1\\le b\\le t+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the triangular configuration defined for the integer \\(t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### What can the sunny lines do?\n\nBy Lemma 2 each sunny line can meet each of the remaining columns in at most one point, therefore it can contribute at most \\(t\\) new points.  With \\(t\\) sunny lines the total number of new points that can be supplied is at most \\(t^{2}\\).\n\nConsequently a necessary condition for covering all points is  \n\n\\[\nt^{2}\\ge U(t)=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2},\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(2t\\ge t+1\\), i.e. \\(t\\ge 1\\).  This condition is always satisfied for \\(t\\ge 1\\); however we must also respect the *sunny* restriction.\n\nThus the problem reduces to the following sub‑problem:\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  For a given integer \\(t\\ge 1\\) can the set \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) be covered by exactly \\(t\\) lines, none of which is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility for \\(t\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume \\(t\\ge 2\\) and that such a covering exists.  \nIn \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) there are exactly \\(t\\) rows, \\(t\\) columns and \\(t\\) anti‑diagonals.  \nEach sunny line can intersect each of these three families in at most one point (Lemma 2).  \n\nSince we have only \\(t\\) sunny lines, to cover all points each row, each column and each anti‑diagonal must be intersected **exactly once** by the collection of sunny lines.  Hence the set of sunny lines induces three bijections:\n\n* a bijection between the set of sunny lines and the set of rows,\n* a bijection between the set of sunny lines and the set of columns,\n* a bijection between the set of sunny lines and the set of anti‑diagonals.\n\nPick two distinct rows, say \\(R_{b_1}\\) and \\(R_{b_2}\\) with \\(b_1<b_2\\).  \nThe unique sunny line meeting \\(R_{b_1}\\) also meets a unique column, say \\(C_{a_1}\\), and a unique anti‑diagonal, say \\(D_{c_1}\\).  \nSimilarly the line meeting \\(R_{b_2}\\) meets a column \\(C_{a_2}\\) and an anti‑diagonal \\(D_{c_2}\\).  \nBecause the two rows are distinct, the two lines are distinct, and therefore \\(\\{a_1,a_2\\}\\) and \\(\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\) are disjoint subsets of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\).\n\nWhen \\(t=2\\) this is impossible: the set \\(\\{1,2\\}\\) cannot contain two disjoint 2‑element subsets.  Hence no covering exists for \\(t=2\\).\n\nFor \\(t\\ge 3\\) the same reasoning applied to any three distinct rows forces the three corresponding columns (and likewise the three anti‑diagonals) to be pairwise disjoint, which cannot happen inside a set of size \\(t\\).  Thus a covering is impossible for every \\(t\\ge 2\\).\n\nConsequently **no configuration with \\(t\\ge 2\\) sunny lines can satisfy the required covering**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(t=0\\) and \\(t=1\\)\n\n*\\(t=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) has its \\(x\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), so it lies on exactly one of these lines.  None of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n*\\(t=1\\).*  \nUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (these are \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines).  \nThe only points not yet covered belong to the last column \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\).  \nCover this single point with a sunny line, for instance  \n\n\\[\ny-1=2\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe line is neither vertical, horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are now covered, and exactly one line is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is also realizable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify