red to have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\), and it cannot be negative. Hence \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is a necessary condition.  \n   - The construction above shows that every integer in this interval is indeed attainable, establishing sufficiency.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely all integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nA line is either sunny or belongs to the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\text{horizontal lines }y=c,\\; \\text{vertical lines }x=c,\\; \\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must lie in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sufficient condition – construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) in the above range.\n\n#### 2.1.  Non‑sunny lines\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\]\n\nPlace the following \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i^{\\text{v}}:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m)\n\\]\n\nand the following \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j^{\\text{h}}:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(2m>n\\) we keep only the first \\(n-k\\) of these \\(2m\\) lines (any selection of \\(n-k\\) distinct lines among them will do).  \nAll these lines belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\), hence are **not** sunny.\n\n#### 2.2.  Coverage of \\(T_n\\) by the non‑sunny lines\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{either } a\\le m \\quad\\text{or}\\quad b\\le m .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if both \\(a>m\\) and \\(b>m\\) held, then  