            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;x=0\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=0\\quad(\\text{diagonal }-45^\\circ).\n\\]\n\nHence every line belongs either to the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\text{horizontal},\\text{vertical},\\text{diagonal }x+y=0\\}\n\\]\n\nor is sunny.  \nIf we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must lie in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\); consequently none of them is sunny.\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m=n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points already covered\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa at most \\(m\\) is already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The remaining region\n\nAssume now that \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a>m\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus all uncovered points belong to the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Sunny lines covering \\(R\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = x + (t-m-1).\n\\]\n\n*The slope of \\(S_t\\) is \\(1\\); it is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), so each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.*  \nMoreover \\(S_t\\) passes through the point \\((m+1,t)\\) because  \n\n\\[\nt = (m+1)+(t-m-1).\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for any \\((a,b)\\in R\\) we have \\(b\\le k\\) and \\(a\\ge m+1\\); setting \\(t=b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = a + (b-m-1) = a + (t-m-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of \\(S_t\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_b\\).\n\nThus the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole rectangle \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Verification of the whole construction\n\nThe total family of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal V|=m=n-k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\); therefore \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny, while those in \\(\\mathcal V\\) are not; hence exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) it lies on a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal V\\).  \nIf \\(a>m\\) then \\((a,b)\\in R\\) and, as shown in §2.4, it lies on the sunny line \\(S_b\\in\\mathcal S\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll required conditions are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Necessarily \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the construction above).\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
