equently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction uses three families of lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1 Two mandatory non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1,\\qquad H:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (vertical and horizontal respectively).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2 \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(2\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=2x-(2i-1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\); its slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so each \\(S_i\\) is sunny. The intercepts \\(-(2i-1)\\) are distinct, hence the \\(S_i\\) are distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3 \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nJ=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\), the set \\(J\\) has exactly \\(n-2-k\\) elements.  \nFor each \\(j\\in J\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_j:\\; x+y=j .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny. Different values of \\(j\\) give different lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.4 Verification that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered  \n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).  \n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n\nAssume now that \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer  \n\n\\[\ns:=a-b\n\\]\n\nsatisfies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le s\\le a-1\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nTwo cases occur.\n\n1. **\\(1\\le s\\le k\\).**  \n   Then \\(b=2a-(2s-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line\n   \\(S_s\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in S_s\\).\n\n2. **\\(s\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).**  \n   By definition of \\(J\\) we have \\(s\\in J\\), and the line \\(D_s\\) is given by\n   \\(x+y=s\\).  Since \\(a+b = a+(a-s)=2a-s\\) and \\(a-b=s\\), we obtain \\(a+b=s\\),\n   so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_s\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5 Counting sunny lines  \n\nThe only sunny lines in the construction are the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\nAll the other lines (\\(V\\), \\(H\\) and the \\(D_j\\)’s) are non‑sunny.  \nHence the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all\nthese values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom the necessity proved in §1 we have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom the explicit construction of §2 we have that every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realized.  \n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
