            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nIf a line is sunny, its slope is finite and different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore it cannot be vertical. Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(C\\), because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate would force the line to be vertical.  \n\nSince \\(|C|=n\\), covering all points of \\(C\\) requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines unless one of the lines is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny. Hence any admissible configuration must contain the line \\(V\\).\n\nNow look at the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line cannot be horizontal, so it can contain at most one point of \\(R\\). As before, covering all points of \\(R\\) without using a horizontal line would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, which is impossible because on