satisfies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le s\\le a-1\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nTwo cases occur.\n\n1. **\\(1\\le s\\le k\\).**  \n   Then \\(b=2a-(2s-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line\n   \\(S_s\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in S_s\\).\n\n2. **\\(s\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).**  \n   By definition of \\(J\\) we have \\(s\\in J\\), and the line \\(D_s\\) is given by\n   \\(x+y=s\\).  Since \\(a+b = a+(a-s)=2a-s\\) and \\(a-b=s\\), we obtain \\(a+b=s\\),\n   so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_s\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5 Counting sunny lines  \n\nThe only sunny lines in the construction are the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\nAll the other lines (\\(V\\), \\(H\\) and the \\(D_j\\)’s) are non‑sunny.  \nHence the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all\nthese values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom the necessity proved in §1 we have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom the explicit construction of §2 we have that every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realized.  \n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
