ints already covered\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line\n\\(V_a\\).  Therefore every point with abscissa at most \\(m\\) is already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The remaining region\n\nAssume now that \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a>m\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence all points not yet covered belong to  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(R\\) is a right‑triangle whose vertices are\n\\((m+1,k),\\;(n,1),\\;(m+1,1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Sunny lines covering \\(R\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = t\\,x-(t\\,m+t).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_t\\) equals \\(t\\); consequently each \\(S_t\\) is sunny\n(it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)).  The collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of \\(R\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in R\\).  Write \\(a=m+u\\) with \\(1\\le u\\le k\\); then by (10)\n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-a = k+1-u .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(t=u\\) (so \\(1\\le t\\le k\\)).  Substituting \\(a=m+u\\) and \\(t=u\\) into\n(11) gives\n\n\\[\ny = u\\,(m+u)-(u\\,m+u)=u .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(S_u\\) passes through the point \\((m+u,u)\\), i.e. the point of\nthe **hypotenuse** of the triangle.\n\nNow consider the points of the same column \\(x=m+u\\) with smaller ordinate.\nFor any integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le u-1\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= m+u+j,\\\\\ny &= u\\,(m+u+j)-(u\\,m+u)=u+j .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence the point \\((m+u+j,\\;u-j)\\) also lies on \\(S_u\\).  Varying \\(j\\) from\n\\(0\\) to \\(u-1\\) yields exactly the set\n\n\\[\n\\{(m+u,\\,u),\\;(m+u+1,\\,u-1),\\dots ,(m+2u-1,\\,1)\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nAll points of column \\(x=m+u\\) whose ordinate satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le u\\) are\npresent in (14).  Because (12) guarantees \\(b\\le k+1-u\\le u\\), every point\n\\((a,b)\\in R\\) belongs to the line \\(S_u\\) with \\(u=a-m\\).\n\nConsequently each point of the region \\(R\\) is covered by exactly one of the\nsunny lines \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Verification of the whole construction\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal V|=m=n-k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\); therefore \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny, while those in \\(\\mathcal V\\) are not;\nhence exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) the point lies on \\(V_a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\nIf \\(a>m\\) then \\((a,b)\\in R\\) and, as proved in §2.4, it lies on the\nsunny line \\(S_{a-m}\\in\\mathcal S\\).  Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs\nto at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll required conditions are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (9) together with the explicit construction of\n§2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions ab