he line \\(x+y=a+b\\), so the covering condition holds.\n\n2. **General \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Start from the construction for \\(k=0\\). Replace \\(k\\) of the lines \\(x+y=t\\) by sunny lines that still pass through the same set of lattice points.  \n   For each chosen integer \\(t\\) we replace the line \\(x+y=t\\) by the line through the two extreme points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,1)\\).  \n   This new line has slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{t-2}{2-t}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); hence it is sunny.  \n   The replacement does not affect the covering of any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) because those points already lie on the original line \\(x+y=t\\) and the new line contains exactly the same integer points of the triangle (the only integer points on \\(x+y=t\\) are those with \\(1\\le a,b\\le t-1\\)).  \n   Performing this replacement for any \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(t\\) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still covering all required points.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists, while \\(k=1\\) is impossible. This completes the classification.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Impossibility of exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that exactly one of them, say \\(L_{1}\\), is sunny.  \nAll other lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; we partition them accordingly:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal H\\): horizontal lines (slope \\(0\\));\n* \\(\\mathcal V\\): vertical lines (slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* \\(\\mathcal D\\): lines of slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\qquad v=|\\mathcal V|,\\qquad d=|\\mathcal D|\n\\]\n\nso that \\(h+v+d=n-1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the **layer**\n\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{s}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe layers are disjoint and \\(|\\Lambda_{s}|=s-1\\).  \nObserve:\n\n* A horizontal line can intersect at most one point of each layer \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\) (its \\(y\\)-coordinate is fixed);\n* A vertical line can intersect at most one point of each layer \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\) (its \\(x\\)-coordinate is fixed);\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) can intersect at most one point of each layer \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\) (its equation is \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\)).\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nh\\,(n-1)+v\\,(n-1)+d\\,(n-1)=(h+v+d)(n-1)=(n-1)(n-1)= (n-1)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)^{2}= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2n^{2}+4n-2}{2}= \\frac{-n^{2}+5n-2}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the non\u2011sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\); at least one point remains uncovered.  \nThat uncovered point must lie on the unique sunny line \\(L_{1}\\).  \nSince a sunny line can intersect at most one point of each layer, it can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThus the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)^{2}+n = n^{2}-2n+1+n = n^{2}-n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-n+1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (indeed \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}- (n^{2}-n+1)=\\frac{-n^{2}+3n-2}{2}>0\\)), we obtain a contradiction.  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) take the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; x+y=t .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) such lines, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \nThus the covering condition holds and we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n##### 2.2  Replacing a line by a sunny one  \n\nFix a particular integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\).  \nConsider the two extreme lattice points of the layer \\(\\Lambda_{t}\\),\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,t-1),\\qquad P_{2}=(t-1,1).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL^{\\ast}_{t}:\\; (t-2)x+(t-2)y=2(t-1) .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{t-2}{2-t}= -1+\\frac{2}{2-t},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(t\\ge 2\\); hence \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe integer points of \\(\\Lambda_{t}\\) are precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le t-1\\).  \nAll of them lie on the segment joining \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\), and therefore belong to the line \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\).  \nConsequently replacing the original line \\(x+y=t\\) by \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) does **not** change the set of covered points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n##### 2.3  Obtaining any \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) replace the line \\(x+y=t\\) by the sunny line \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) described above; for the remaining values of \\(t\\) keep the original line \\(x+y=t\\).  \n\nThe resulting family consists of\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) for \\(t\\in S\\));\n* \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the unchanged lines \\(x+y=t\\) for \\(t\\notin S\\));\n\nand still covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\) because each layer \\(\\Lambda_{t}\\) is covered either by \\(x+y=t\\) or by \\(L^{\\ast}