).  \n   The column contains the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a).\n   \\]\n\n   The topmost point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   The bottom point \\((a,1)\\) belongs to the bottom row \\(B\\).  \n   All intermediate points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-a\\) belong to \\(M\\).\n\n   *Claim.*  In any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines, the column \\(x=a\\) must contain a **vertical** line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n   *Proof of claim.*  \n   Suppose the column does **not** contain the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   The diagonal already covers the top point \\((a,n+1-a)\\).  \n   Any other line that passes through an intermediate point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-a\\) is not vertical; therefore it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in exactly that point and cannot meet the same column elsewhere.  \n   Consequently each intermediate point would require a distinct non\u2011vertical line.  \n   There are \\(n-a-1\\ge 1\\) such intermediate points, so at least \\(n-a-1\\) distinct non\u2011vertical lines would be needed **in addition** to the diagonal and the horizontal line (see step\u202f5).  \n   Adding the diagonal, the horizontal line (which will be needed for the bottom row, see step\u202f5) and the already existing lines for the other columns would give more than \\(n\\) lines, contradicting the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used.  \n   Hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) must be present. \u220e\n\n   Because the argument works for every \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), the family must contain the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_a:\\;x=a\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Covering the bottom row.**  \n   The bottom row \\(B\\) consists of the points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   For \\(a\\le n-2\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) already lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n   The remaining two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) are not on any of the vertical lines listed in step\u202f4.  \n   Both are covered by the **horizontal** line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(0\\)).  Thus the bottom row is completely covered.\n\n6. **The forced set of lines.**  \n   Collecting the lines obtained in steps\u202f3\u20135 we have exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   L_0:\\;x+y=n+1,\\qquad\n   H:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n   V_a:\\;x=a\\;(a=1,\\dots ,n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   Their number is  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{diagonal})+1\\;(\\text{horizontal})+(n-2)\\;(\\text{vertical})=n.\n   \\]\n\n   All of them are **non\u2011sunny** (slopes \\(-1,0,\\infty\\) respectively).  \n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D\\) and lies on \\(L_0\\).  \n   * Else if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in B\\) and lies on \\(H\\).  \n   * Otherwise \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\le n-2\\); then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the column \\(x=a\\) and is on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   Hence every required point belongs to at least one line of the family.\n\n7. **Absence of sunny lines.**  \n   Steps\u202f2\u20134 show that any line that is not vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) can cover at most one point of the diagonal and at most one point of each column.  \n   Because the diagonal contains \\(n-1\\) points, covering all of them would already require at least \\(n-1\\) distinct lines.  \n   Adding the necessity of a horizontal line for the two uncovered bottom\u2011row points forces the total number of lines to be at least \\(n+1\\) if any sunny line is used, contradicting the requirement of exactly \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Therefore **no sunny line can appear** in any admissible configuration; i.e. the number of sunny lines must be \\(k=0\\).\n\n8. **Existence.**  \n   The explicit family displayed in step\u202f6 satisfies all conditions for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe partition \\(P_n\\) as follows.\n\n*Diagonal.*  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(a,b)\\in P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |D|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Bottom row.*  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(a,1)\\in P_n\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |B|=n .\n\\]\n\n*Middle points.*  \n\n\\[\nM=P_n\\setminus(D\\cup B)\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-2,\\;2\\le b\\le n-a\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Properties of sunny lines  \n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n\n*Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D\\).  \n\n*Proof.* Any two distinct points of \\(D\\) have coordinates \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and \\((a',n+1-a')\\) with \\(a\\neq a'\\); the line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-a')-(n+1-a)}{a'-a}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus a line through two diagonal points has slope \\(-1\\) and is not sunny. \u220e\n\n*Lemma 2.* A sunny line meets a fixed vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  \n\n*Proof.* A non\u2011vertical line is the graph of a linear function \\(y=mx+b\\) with a unique \\(y\\) for each \\(x\\); hence it intersects the line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessity of the diagonal line  \n\nBecause \\(|D|=n-1\\) and by Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can cover at most one diagonal point, at least \\(n-1\\) distinct lines are required to cover all points of \\(D\\).  \n\nThe line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-1\\) (hence is non\u2011sunny) and contains **all** points of \\(D\\).  \nTherefore any admissible family of exactly \\(n\\) lines may replace the \\(n-1\\) hypothetical sunny lines by the single line \\(L_0\\) together with at most \\(n-2\\) further lines, each of which must intersect \\(L_0\\) in a *different* diagonal point (otherwise some diagonal point would stay uncovered).  \n\nThus \\(L_0\\) is forced to belong to every admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of vertical lines  \n\nFix \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-2\\).  \nThe column \\(x=a\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a).\n\\]\n\nThe top point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) lies on \\(L_0\\).  \nAll other points of the column belong to \\(B\\) (the bottom point) or to \\(M\\) (the intermediate points).  \n\nAssume that the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is **absent** from the family.  \n\n* The bottom point \\((a,1)\\) would then have to be covered by a non\u2011vertical line; such a line meets the column only at that point.  \n\n* Each intermediate point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-a\\) also would need a distinct non\u2011vertical line, because any non\u2011vertical line can intersect the column in at most one point (Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nHence, without the vertical line \\(x=a\\), we would need at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{for }(a,1))+(n-a-1)\\;(\\text{for the intermediate points})\n   =n-a\n\\]\n\ndifferent non\u2011vertical lines in addition to the already required diagonal line \\(L_0\\).  \n\nSumming over all columns \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) would give more than \\(n\\) lines in total, contradicting the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used.  \n\nTherefore the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a\\qquad (a=1,\\dots ,n-2)\n\\]\n\nmust be present in every admissible family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Covering the bottom row  \n\nFor \\(a\\le n-2\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) is already on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \nThe two remaining points of the bottom row are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \nBoth are covered by the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(0\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The forced set of lines  \n\nCollecting the lines obtained in Sections\u202f2\u20134 we obtain the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,L_0:x+y=n+1,\\;\n        H:y=1,\\;\n        V_a:x=a\\;(a=1,\\dots ,n-2)\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n1+1+(n-2)=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny (slopes \\(-1,0,\\infty\\) respectively).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers all required points  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in D\\) and lies on \\(L_0\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in B\\) and lies on \\(H\\).\n\n*Otherwise* we have \\(b\\ge 2\\) and, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), necessarily \\(a\\le n-2\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the column \\(x=a\\) and lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Impossibility of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose a configuration satisfying the statement contained a sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most one diagonal point, so at least \\(n-2\\) additional lines are needed to cover the remaining \\(n-2\\) diagonal points (each of them must meet the diagonal in a distinct point).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f2, \\(\\ell\\) can intersect each column \\(x=a\\) in at most one point; consequently, to cover the intermediate points of the columns we still need a vertical line for every column \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n\nTogether with a horizontal line for the two uncovered bottom\u2011row points, the total number of distinct lines would be at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{the sunny line})+(n-2)\\;(\\text{other diagonal\u2011covering lines})\n   +(n-2)\\;(\\text{vertical lines})+1\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;=\\;2n-2>n,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts