d non‑vertical lines\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{n-2}(n-a-1)=|M|.\n\\]\n\nBut the set \\(M\\) itself consists of exactly \\(|M|\\) points, and we already have the two mandatory lines \\(L_0\\) and \\(H\\).  \nWith only \\(n\\) lines available there is no room for \\(|M|\\) additional distinct non‑vertical lines; the only way to meet the covering requirement is to **use a vertical line for each column**.  \nThus for every \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a\n\\]\n\nmust belong to the family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The forced family of non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\bigl\\{L_0,\\;H,\\;V_a\\;(a=1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|\\mathcal L_0|=1+1+(n-2)=n\\).  \nAll its slopes are \\(-1,0,\\infty\\); therefore every line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is non‑sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D\\) and lies on \\(L_0\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in B\\) and lies on \\(H\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(2\\le b\\le n-a\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n-2\\); such a point belongs to the column \\(x=a\\) and is covered by the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(P_n\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThus a configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nThe two lines \\(L_0\\) and \\(H\\) are indispensable, but the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines may be replaced by sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\nFor each column \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n-2\\)) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_a:\\; y=\\frac12\\,(x-a)+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(S_a\\) is sunny.  \n\\(S_a\\) passes through the points \\((a,2)\\) and \\((a+2,1)\\); both belong to \\(P_n\\) because \\(a+2\\le n\\).\n\nObserve:\n\n* The point \\((a,2)\\) (previously covered by \\(V_a\\)) is now covered by \\(S_a\\).\n* The point \\((a+2,1)\\) is already covered by the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n* All other points of column \\(x=a\\) are either the top point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) on \\(L_0\\) or, if we have not replaced \\(V_a\\), are still covered by the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nConsequently, if we **replace any subset** of the vertical lines \\(\\{V_a\\}\\) by the corresponding sunny lines \\(\\{S_a\\}\\), the resulting collection still consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and still covers every point of \\(P_n\\).\n\nThe number of sunny lines in the new family equals the number of replaced verticals.  \nSince we may replace anywhere from \\(0\\) up to all \\(n-2\\) verticals, we obtain configurations with every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Upper bound revisited\n\nStep 1 proved that at least two lines must be non‑sunny, so no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \nCombined with the construction of Step 4, the set of attainable values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
