including the interior points). Its size is\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum points on a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\), giving at most \\(n\\) integer values for \\(a\\). The same bound holds for a vertical line. For a line of slope \\(-1\\) we have the equation \\(x+y=c\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) implies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); for a fixed \\(c\\) the points of \\(S\\) on this line are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\), i.e. exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny lines meet \\(S\\) in at most one point)\n\nIf a line is sunny, then it contains at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S\\). The slope of \\(L\\) is the rational number \\(\\dfrac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\). The only rational slopes that are forbidden are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\). Since \\(L\\) is sunny, its slope cannot be any of these three values. Hence the existence of two distinct lattice points on a sunny line would force the slope to be one of the forbidden values, a contradiction. Therefore a sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most one point. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nSuppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them a