 \nEvaluating the equation of \\(M_a\\) at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{a+1}=a .\n\\]\n\nHence the point \\((a,a)\\) lies on \\(M_a\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le a\\) (the last inequality follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge 1\\)), we have \\(b\\le a\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies the equation of \\(M_a\\) (the line contains all points with abscissa \\(a\\) and ordinate \\(\\le a\\)).  \nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{M_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\).  \nTherefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nDefine two families of lines:\n\n* **Vertical (non‑sunny) lines**  \n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines**  \n  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(n-k+1\\le i\\le n\\) set  \n  \\[\n  M_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n  \\]\n\nAll \\(L_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny; all \\(M_i\\) have slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\), hence are sunny.  \nThe slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are distinct, so the \\(M_i\\) are distinct; together with the distinct vertical lines we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification that the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen the point lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\) because that line is defined by \\(x=a\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).*  \nConsider the sunny line \\(M_a\\) (which exists because \\(a\\le n\\)).  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(M_a\\) yields \\(y=a\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\ge 1\\), we have \\(n+1-a\\le a\\); therefore \\(b\\le a\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(M_a\\) (the line contains every point with abscissa \\(a\\) and ordinate not exceeding \\(a\\)).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(M_{n-k+1},\\dots ,M_n\\), all of which are sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, which are non‑sunny.  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines in the configuration is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConversely, any configuration must contain a non‑negative integer number of sunny lines, so no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
