            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\).  \nFor a lattice point we write \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}\\).  \nThe set that has to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Extremal constructions  \n\n#### Lemma 2.1  (No sunny line, \\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines; each is either vertical or has slope \\(-1\\), hence none is sunny.\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-1\\)* then \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n*If \\(a=n\\)* then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2  (All lines sunny, \\(k=n\\))\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+i .\n\\]\n\nAll slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are different from \\(0,-1\\) and are finite, so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\). Put  \n\n\\[\nt=n+1-a-b\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n-2).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni=a\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1),\\qquad j=n-t\\;(1\\le j\\le n).\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_j\\):\n\\[\nb=\\frac{j}{j+1}a+j\n   =\\frac{n-t}{n-t+1}a+n-t\n   =\\frac{n+1-a-b}{n-a-b+2}\\,a+n-a-b .\n\\]\nSince \\(a+b=n+1-t\\) this identity holds, hence \\((a,b)\\in L_j\\).  \nTherefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall build a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nTake the following \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;V_i:\\;x=i\\quad (1\\le i\\le n-k)\\;},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;D:\\;x+y=n+1\\;}.\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)) and \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny.  \nTogether they are \\(n-k+1\\) lines.  \nIf \\(k=n\\) we simply omit this step (the construction of §2.2 is used instead).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Points not covered by the non‑sunny lines  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) fails to lie on any \\(V_i\\) precisely when \\(a>n-k\\).  \nIf additionally \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on \\(D\\); otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nHence the uncovered points are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(i\\ge 1\\) and using \\(a+b\\le n\\) gives \\(b\\le k-i\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) is exactly the lattice triangle with vertices  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(n-k+i,\\;i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nand right‑angle at \\((n-k+1,1)\\).  Its side length (in lattice steps) is \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define a line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;S_i:\\; y = \\alpha_i x + \\beta_i\\;},\n\\]\nwhere the slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) is chosen arbitrarily subject to  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j),\n\\]\n\nand the intercept \\(\\beta_i\\) is then forced by requiring that \\(S_i\\) pass through the point \\(P_i\\):\n\n\\[\n\\beta_i = i-\\alpha_i\\,(n-k+i).\n\\]\n\nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct.  \nEach \\(\\alpha_i\\) is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n\n*Claim.* Every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) lies on one of the \\(S_i\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_k\\). Write \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(b\\le k-r\\).  \nConsider the line \\(S_r\\). By construction it contains the point \\(P_r=(n-k+r,r)\\).  \nBecause the slope \\(\\alpha_r\\) is the same for all points on \\(S_r\\),\n\n\\[\nb = \\alpha_r a + \\beta_r\n   = \\alpha_r (n-k+r) + \\bigl(r-\\alpha_r (n-k+r)\\bigr)\n   = r .\n\\]\n\nThus the only point of \\(S_r\\) with abscissa \\(a=n-k+r\\) is \\((a,r)\\).  \nSince \\(b\\le k-r\\) and the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) have ordinates exactly \\(1,2,\\dots ,k-r\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) coincides with \\((a,r)\\) only when \\(b=r\\).  \nIf \\(b\\neq r\\) then \\(a\\) must be smaller; i.e. the point belongs to a line \\(S_{b}\\) (because its ordinate equals the index of the line that passes through \\((n-k+b,b)\\)).  \nHence every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_k\\) lies on the unique line \\(S_{b}\\). ∎\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) together cover the whole triangle \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4  The final family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k},D\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}, & 0\\le k\\le n-1,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}                              & k=n,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nwhere the \\(L_i\\) are the sunny lines of Lemma 2.2.  \n\n*Number of lines.*  \nFor \\(k\\le n-1\\) we have \\((n-k)\\) vertical lines, one diagonal line, and \\(k\\) sunny lines, totalling \\(n\\).  \nFor \\(k=n\\) we have exactly \\(n\\) lines by construction.\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nIn the first case the only sunny lines are the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\); the vertical and diagonal lines are not sunny.  \nIn the second case all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf \\(a>n-k\\) then either \\(a+b=n+1\\) (the point lies on \\(D\\)) or \\(a+b\\le n\\) (the point belongs to \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) and is covered by the appropriate \\(S_i\\) by the claim proved in §3.3).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\nAll requirements of the problem are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above.