q}\\) written in lowest terms, where \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\). Then the number of points of \\(S\\) lying on this line satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap S\\bigr)\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) lie on the line, their difference vector is an integer multiple of \\((q,p)\\); indeed the direction vector of a line of slope \\(p/q\\) is \\((q,p)\\). Hence all lattice points of the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\((q,p)\\). Starting from any point of the progression, moving \\(t\\) steps in the positive direction changes the coordinates by \\((tq,tp)\\). To stay inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we must have \\(t\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\le n\\). Therefore the number of admissible integer steps \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), and together with the initial point we obtain (2). ∎\n\nBecause a sunny line is defined by a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), we have \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap S\\bigr)\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\n- Each of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (Lemma 1).  \n- Each sunny line can cover at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S\\) (Lemma 2).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|S|\\;\\le\\;(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nInsert (1) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-kn+k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{∗}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### What remains to be shown\n\nThe inequality (∗) gives a necessary condition on the number of sunny lines.  \nTo solve the original problem one must also prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) can actually be realized, i.e. exhibit, for each such \\(k\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. The simple construction based on replacing some diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) by sunny lines does not work because a sunny line may pass through several points of \\(S\\), violating the counting argument that led to (∗). A correct construction (or a proof that some values of \\(k\\) below the bound are impossible) is still missing.\n\nHence the problem is solved up to the upper bound (∗); the converse direction remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
