gr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) lie on the line, their difference vector is an integer multiple of the primitive direction vector \\((q,p)\\); indeed the line consists of all points \\((x_{0},y_{0})+t(q,p)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \n\nConsequently every lattice point of the line can be written as  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_{0}+tq,\\;y_{0}+tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nfor some fixed lattice point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) on the line.  \n\nFor such a point we have  \n\n\\[\nx+y = (x_{0}+y_{0})+t(q+p).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|q|\\le m\\) and \\(|p|\\le m\\), moving one step (\\(t\\to t+1\\)) changes each coordinate by at most \\(m\\) in absolute value, and changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by at most \\(2m\\).  \n\nAll points of \\(S\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), \\(1\\le y\\le n\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\). Starting from any point of the line that lies in \\(S\\), we may increase \\(t\\) only while both coordinates stay \\(\\le n\\) and the sum stays \\(\\le n+1\\). Each increase of \\(t\\) raises the larger of the two coordinates by at least \\(1\\) and at most \\(m\\); therefore after at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/m\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps one of the coordinates would exceed \\(n\\) or the sum would exceed \\(n+1\\). The same bound holds when decreasing \\(t\\). Hence at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{m}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent integer values of \\(t\\) can give points inside \\(S\\), which proves (2). ∎\n\n\n\nBecause a sunny line has \\(m\\ge 2\\), (2) yields the simpler estimate  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap S\\bigr)\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(S\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n* From Lemma 1 each of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines covers at mos