k\\ge 0\\) so the families in (4) are well\u2011defined).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and inequality (3) give the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). The explicit construction above shows that this condition is also sufficient. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) are precisely the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are possible. The same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\) it has equation \\(x+y=c\\). The constraints \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) give \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). For a fixed \\(c\\) the points of \\(S\\) on this line are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), i.e. exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Points on a sunny line)\n\nLet a line have reduced slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\). Put  \n\n\\[\nm=\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ (\\ge 2).\n\\]\n\nThen the number of points of \\(S\\) lying on this line satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap S\\bigr)\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{m}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) lie on the line, their difference vector is an integer multiple of the primitive direction vector \\((q,p)\\); indeed the line consists of all points \\((x_{0},y_{0})+t(q,p)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \n\nConsequently every lattice point of the line can be written as  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_{0}+tq,\\;y_{0}+tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nfor some fixed lattice point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) on the line.  \n\nFor such a point we have  \n\n\\[\nx+y = (x_{0}+y_{0})+t(q+p).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|q|\\le m\\) and \\(|p|\\le m\\), moving one step (\\(t\\to t+1\\)) changes each coordinate by at most \\(m\\) in absolute value, and changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by at most \\(2m\\).  \n\nAll points of \\(S\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), \\(1\\le y\\le n\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\). Starting from any point of the line that lies in \\(S\\), we may increase \\(t\\) only while both coordinates stay \\(\\le n\\) and the sum stays \\(\\le n+1\\). Each increase of \\(t\\) raises the larger of the two coordinates by at least \\(1\\) and at most \\(m\\); therefore after at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/m\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps one of the coordinates would exceed \\(n\\) or the sum would exceed \\(n+1\\). The same bound holds when decreasing \\(t\\). Hence at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{m}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent integer values of \\(t\\) can give points inside \\(S\\), which proves (2). \u220e\n\n\n\nBecause a sunny line has \\(m\\ge 2\\), (2) yields the simpler estimate  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap S\\bigr)\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(S\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n* From Lemma\u202f1 each of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n* From (3) each sunny line covers at most \\(1+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\Bigl(1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nInsert (1) and rearrange:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-kn+k\\Bigl(1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nr=n-2k\\ (\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\n**Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the following \\(2r\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r,\\\\\n&y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=r,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand the \\(k\\) diagonal lines\n\n\\[\nx+y=r+2,\\;x+y=r+3,\\dots ,x+y=r+k+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (5)\u2013(6) are vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.  \nA direct inspection shows that the union of these lines covers every point of \\(S\\) **except** the \\(k\\) points\n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(r+i,\\;i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(b\\le r\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (from (5)) contains it; if \\(a\\le r\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (from (5)) contains it; otherwise \\(a>r\\) and \\(b>0\\) with \\(a+b\\le r+k+1\\), so \\(a+b\\) equals one of the numbers \\(r+2,\\dots ,r+k+1\\) and the corresponding diagonal line (6) contains the point. The only points not captured by this reasoning are precisely the \\(P_{i}\\) of (7).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) introduce the line\n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y=\\frac{i}{\\,r+i\\,}x .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{r+i}\\) is a reduced fraction whose denominator is at least \\(2\\) (because \\(r\\ge0\\) and \\(i\\ge1\\)), so \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(L_{i}\\) passes through \\((r+i,i)=P_{i}\\). If another point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lay on \\(L_{i}\\), then \\((a,b)=t(r+i,i)\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge1\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+2k+1\\) forces \\(t=1\\); consequently \\(P_{i}\\) is the **only** point of \\(S\\) on \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n**Counting the lines.**  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{r}_{\\text{vertical}}+\\underbrace{r}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{diagonal}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=2r+2k=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct because they have different slopes or different intercepts. Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (4) together with the explicit construction shows that the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nHence the problem is completely solved."
