at every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable.**  \n   - **Step 1 (non‑sunny backbone).**  \n     Use the \\(n-2k\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2k,\\\\\n     &y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2k,\\\\\n     &x+y=n-2k+2,\\;x+y=n-2k+3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     These are respectively vertical, horizontal and diagonal (\\(slope=-1\\)) lines; all are non‑sunny.  \n     One checks directly that the union of the lines in (4) covers every point of \\(S\\) **except** the \\(k\\) points  \n\n     \\[\n     P_{i}=(n-2k+i,\\;i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k). \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Step 2 (adding sunny lines).**  \n     For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\ introduce the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{\\,n-2k+i\\,}\\,x .\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n     The slope \\(\\frac{i}{n-2k+i}\\) is a reduced fraction with denominator at least \\(2\\) (because \\(n-2k\\ge 0\\) and \\(i\\ge1\\)), so \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the point \\(P_{i}\\) and through no other point of \\(S\\): if \\((a,b)\\in S\\) also lay on \\(L_{i}\\) then \\((a,b)=t\\,(n-2k+i,i)\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge1\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(t=1\\), giving exactly \\(P_{i}\\).\n\n   - **Step 3 (verification).**  \n     The total number of lines is  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-2k)+(n-2k)+(k)=n,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the \\(n-2k\\) vertical, the \\(n-2k\\) horizontal, the \\(k\\) diagonal lines from (4) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{i}\\).  \n     By construction every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny. All lines are distinct because they have different slopes or different intercepts.\n\n   - This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) (the inequality guarantees that \\(n-2k\\ge 0\\) so the families in (4) are