            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) are the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1\nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\le n\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are possible. The same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\) it has equation \\(x+y=c\\). The conditions \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) give \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). For a fixed \\(c\\) the points of \\(S\\) on the line are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), i.e. exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2\nLet a line have reduced slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}=m\\ge2\\) (so the line is sunny). Then\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap S\\bigr)\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{m}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\). Any two lattice points on the line differ by an integer multiple of the primitive direction vector \\((q,p)\\). Hence every lattice point of the line can be expressed as  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_{0},y_{0})+t(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nfor some fixed lattice point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) on the line.  \n\nBecause \\(|q|\\le m\\) and \\(|p|\\le m\\), moving from \\(t\\) to \\(t+1\\) changes each coordinate by at most \\(m\\) in absolute value. Starting from a point of \\(S\\), we may increase \\(t\\) only while both coordinates stay \\(\\le n\\) and the sum stays \\(\\le n+1\\). After at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/m\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such steps one of the coordinates would exceed \\(n\\) or the sum would exceed \\(n+1\\); the same bound holds when decreasing \\(t\\). Consequently at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{m}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent integer values of \\(t\\) give points belonging to \\(S\\), which proves (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(S\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nBy Lemma 1 each of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); by (2) each sunny line contains at most \\(1+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points. Hence\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\Bigl(1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nInsert (1) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-kn+k\\Bigl(1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nr=n-2k\\ (\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\n#### 1. Non‑sunny lines (total \\(n-k\\))\n\n*Vertical lines*  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\)*  \n\n\\[\nx+y=r+2,\\;x+y=r+3,\\dots ,x+y=r+k+1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (4)–(5) are non‑sunny.\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le r\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (4) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a>r\\)*, then \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+2k+1\\); therefore \\(a+b\\) equals one of the integers \\(r+2,\\dots ,r+k+1\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) from (5).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines (4)–(5).\n\n#### 2. Sunny lines (exactly \\(k\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{\\,r+i\\,}x . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{r+i}\\) is reduced and its denominator is at least \\(2\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n\\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(r+i,\\;i).\n\\]\n\nIf another lattice point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lay on \\(L_i\\), then \\((a,b)=(r+i,i)+t(q,p)\\) where \\((q,p)=(r+i,i)\\) is the primitive direction vector of \\(L_i\\) and \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\). Because both coordinates must stay between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) and satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+2k+1\\), the only admissible integer is \\(t=0\\); hence \\(P_i\\) is the **only** point of \\(S\\) on \\(L_i\\). The lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct from one another and from all lines in (4)–(5).\n\n#### 3. Counting\n\nNumber of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{r}_{\\text{vertical}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{diagonal}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=r+2k=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nInequality (3) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
