tinct points of \\(V_n\\) is vertical, i.e. has slope \\(\\infty\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(V_n\\).  \nThe set \\(V_n\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points, so covering it requires at least \\(n-1\\) distinct lines that are not sunny.  \nOne of those lines is already the diagonal line used for \\(D_n\\); therefore we need **one more** non\u2011sunny line.\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, and consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions\n\n#### Lemma 1 (a configuration with \\(k=0\\))  \n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i\\) for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\);\n* the horizontal line \\(H:\\;y=1\\);\n* the diagonal line \\(D:\\;x+y=n\\).\n\nAll three families are non\u2011sunny (slopes \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) respectively).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) and the point again lies on the diagonal line.\n* The point \\((1,1)\\) also lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines, and we have a configuration with **no** sunny lines. \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (adding sunny lines)\n\nFor integers \\(p<q\\) with \\(1\\le p<q\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{p,q}:\\; y=\\frac{q-p}{\\,q-p+1\\,}(x-p)+p .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_{p,q}\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{p}= (p,p),\\qquad Q_{q}= (q,q-1),\n\\]\n\nboth of which belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\np+p=2p\\le n+1,\\qquad q+(q-1)=2q-1\\le n+1\\quad (