e two of its points would have the same difference of coordinates, forcing the line to be parallel to one of the forbidden directions). Consequently  \n\n\\[\ns\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,(n-1)\\ge \\frac{(n-1)n}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Construction for the lower bound.*  \nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.\n\n1. **Base configuration (no sunny lines).**  \n   Take the three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{vertical lines }x=1,2,\\dots ,n-2,\\\\\n   &\\text{horizontal lines }y=1,2,\\dots ,n-2,\\\\\n   &\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=3,4,\\dots ,n .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Among these there are exactly \\((n-2)+(n-2)+(n-2)=3n-6\\) lines, far more than needed.  \n   Choose any \\(n\\) of them that together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); for instance, take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) together with the two diagonal lines \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=n\\). One checks directly that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines. All chosen lines are non\u2011sunny, so this yields a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **Adding sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the configuration above, replace one of the non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line that still passes through all points previously covered by that line.  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the set of points with \\(a=i\\) (i.e. the vertical line \\(x=i\\)) is  \n   \\[\n   \\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n   \\]\n   These points are collinear, but they also lie on the sunny line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\); its equation is \\(y = x-i+1\\). This line has slope \\(1\\), which is **not** parallel to any of the forbidden directions, hence it is sunny.  \n   Replacing the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by this sunny line reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one while preserving coverage of all points that were on \\(x=i\\). Repeating this replacement for any \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(i\\) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   Because we may perform the replacement for any subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), we obtain configurations with every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Distinctness of the lines.*  \nThe replacements described above never create duplicate lines: each sunny line introduced has slope \\(1\\) and a distinct intercept, while the remaining non\u2011sunny lines retain distinct equations (different vertical positions, different constants, etc.). Hence the final collection always consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nCombining Step\u202f2 (the upper bound) with Step\u202f3 (the constructive realisation) we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points on a sunny line).  \n\nA sunny line contains at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains \\(m\\) points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a_1<b_1<a_2<b_2<\\dots\\).  \nBecause the points are integer, the differences \\((a_{i+1}-a_i,b_{i+1}-b_i)\\) are integer vectors.  \nIf \\(m\\ge n-1\\), by the pigeon\u2011hole principle two of these differences must be equal, say  \n\n\\[\n(a_{j+1}-a_j,b_{j+1}-b_j)=(a_{k+1}-a_k,b_{k+1}-b_k).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the direction vector of \\(L\\) is proportional to this common difference, which is an integer vector with either zero \\(x\\)\u2011component, zero \\(y\\)\u2011component, or equal components (the only possibilities for a vector joining two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) while staying inside the triangle). Hence \\(L\\) would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions, contradicting the assumption that it is sunny. \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines).  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIf \\(s\\) denotes the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\), then  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the three sides of the triangle each have exactly \\(n-1\\) lattice points).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line contains at most \\(n-2\\) points.  \nHence the total number of incidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,(n-1)=n(n-1)-s .\n\\]\n\nSince each point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, we need  \n\n\\[\nn(n-1)-s\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich yields \\(s\\le n-2\\). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Existence of a configuration with any prescribed \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the following \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L_i^{\\text{V}}: x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\n&L_1^{\\text{D}}: x+y=3,\\\\\n&L_2^{\\text{D}}: x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-2\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n-1\\) (then \\(b\\le 2\\) and the point lies on \\(x+y=3\\)) or \\(a=n\\) (then \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on \\(x+y=n\\)). Thus the lines in (1) indeed cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). All of them are non\u2011sunny.\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) define a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_i\\) passes through the points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\); both belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Moreover \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is not \\(0\\), undefined, nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nReplace, for a chosen subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\), each vertical line \\(L_i^{\\text{V}}\\) (for \\(i\\in I\\)) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \nThe new family consists of  \n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\);\n* the remaining \\((n-2)-k\\) vertical lines \\(\\{L_i^{\\text{V}}\\mid i\\notin I\\}\\);\n* the two diagonal lines \\(L_1^{\\text{D}},L_2^{\\text{D}}\\).\n\nAll together there are \\(k+((n-2)-k)+2=n\\) lines.  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that was previously covered by a removed vertical line \\(x=i\\) is still covered, because that point now lies on \\(S_i\\). Points that were covered by the unchanged lines remain covered. Hence the new family still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nThe lines are distinct: the sunny lines have distinct intercepts \\(-i+1\\), the remaining vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, and the two diagonal lines have equations \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=n\\), none of which coincides with any of the others.  \n\nThus we have produced a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
