\).  This yields \\(m+m+1=n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, and by the discussion above they cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nWrite \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge 2\\).  Choose the \\(m-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m-1,\n\\]\n\nthe \\(m-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=m-1,\n\\]\n\nand the two diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad x+y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\( (m-1)+(m-1)+2 = 2m = n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  Again, every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to one of the selected lines: if \\(a\\le m-1\\) we use the vertical line \\(x=a\\); if \\(b\\le m-1\\) we use the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); otherwise \\(a\\ge m\\) and \\(b\\ge m\\), whence \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+1\\) forces either \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n-1\\), and the corresponding diagonal line contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{is always admissible}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow we must produce \\(n\\) *sunny* lines that together contain all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe idea is to partition \\(S_n\\) into \\(n\\) subsets, each of which lies on a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  Because the subsets are disjoint, the corresponding lines are distinct, and all of them are sunny.\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(P_t\\) are precisely the \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 layers of the integer lattice inside the triangle bounded by the axes and the line \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Each \\(P_t\\) is non\u2011empty and consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=\\{(1,t-1),(2,t-2),\\dots ,(t-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|P_t|=t-1\\).  The total number of points is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|.\n\\]\n\nNow we split each layer \\(P_t\\) into singletons, except that we will merge the first point of each layer with the last point of the previous layer.  More formally, define a sequence of points  \n\n\\[\nQ_1=(1,1),\\qquad \nQ_2=(1,2),\\qquad \nQ_3=(2,1),\\qquad \nQ_4=(1,3),\\qquad \nQ_5=(2,2),\\qquad \nQ_6=(3,1),\\dots\n\\]\n\nobtained by reading the points of \\(S_n\\) row\u2011by\u2011row, i.e. in order of increasing \\(a+b\\) and, within each diagonal, in increasing \\(a\\).  This produces exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points, which we now group into \\(n\\) consecutive blocks of sizes  \n\n\\[\n1,2,3,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nBecause  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|,\n\\]\n\nthe partition is well defined.  Denote the \\(j\\)-th block by  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\{Q_{\\,\\frac{j(j-1)}{2}+1},\\;Q_{\\,\\frac{j(j-1)}{2}+2},\\dots ,Q_{\\,\\frac{j(j+1)}{2}}\\},\n\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nEach block \\(B_j\\) contains exactly \\(j\\) points, and the blocks are pairwise disjoint.\n\n**Claim.** For each \\(j\\) the points of \\(B_j\\) are collinear on a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nWrite the points of \\(B_j\\) explicitly.  By construction the first point of \\(B_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(1,\\,j\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nand the subsequent points are obtained by repeatedly adding the vector \\((1,-1)\\) until we have taken \\(j-1\\) steps.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\bigl\\{(1+j',\\,j-j')\\mid j'=0,1,\\dots ,j-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(B_j\\) satisfy the linear equation  \n\n\\[\n(j+1)\\,x - (j-1)\\,y = 2j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, substituting \\((x,y)=(1+j',\\,j-j')\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(j+1)(1+j')-(j-1)(j-j')\n= (j+1)+(j+1)j'-(j-1)j+(j-1)j'\n=2j .\n\\]\n\nEquation (1) defines a line with slope  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{j+1}{j-1}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j\\ge 2\\) we have \\(m_j\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); for \\(j=1\\) the block \\(B_1\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\), and we may assign to it any sunny line, for instance the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  Thus every block lies on a sunny line, and distinct blocks give distinct slopes, hence distinct lines.  \u220e\n\nTaking for each \\(j\\) the line defined by (1) (or \\(y=x\\) when \\(j=1\\)) we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere each \\(L_j\\) is sunny and contains exactly the points of \\(B_j\\).  Since the blocks partition \\(S_n\\), the union of the \\(L_j\\) covers all required points.  The lines are distinct because their slopes \\(\\frac{j+1}{j-1}\\) are distinct for different \\(j\\) (the case \\(j=1\\) has slope \\(1\\), which does not coincide with any \\(\\frac{j+1}{j-1}\\) for \\(j\\ge 2\\)).  \n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk=n\\quad\\text{is always admissible}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion of the partial result\n\nWe have proved rigorously that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the two extreme values  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare attainable.  The existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with intermediate numbers of sunny lines \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains open.  The constructions above suggest that by replacing some of the non\u2011sunny lines in the \\(k=0\\) configuration with suitable sunny lines (or conversely, by merging some of the sunny lines in the \\(k=n\\) configuration) one may be able to realise additional values of \\(k\\); however a complete classification has not been achieved.  \n\n\u220e"
