            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).  \nWe must determine, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{k\\ge 0\\mid\\exists\\; \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\text{ distinct lines with }\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq S_n\\text{ and exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations for the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n#### 1.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines as follows.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 1\\)).  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m,\n\\]\n\nthe \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=m,\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(m+m+1=n\\) distinct lines, each having slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence all are non‑sunny.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge 2\\)).  \nTake the \\(m-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m-1,\n\\]\n\nthe \\(m-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=m-1,\n\\]\n\nand the two diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n,\\qquad x+y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAgain we have \\((m-1)+(m-1)+2=2m=n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le m\\) (odd case) or \\(a\\le m-1\\) (even case) we use the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n* Else if \\(b\\le m\\) (odd) or \\(b\\le m-1\\) (even) we use the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge m+1\\) and \\(b\\ge m+1\\) (odd) or \\(a\\ge m\\) and \\(b\\ge m\\) (even).  \n  In the odd case \\(a+b=n\\); in the even case \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n-1\\).  \n  Hence the appropriate diagonal line contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\).  \nConsequently \\(0\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n\\) define the set  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\bigl\\{(1+j',\\,j-j')\\mid j'=0,1,\\dots ,j-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(B_1,\\dots ,B_n\\) are pairwise disjoint and\n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}B_j=S_n,\n\\qquad |B_j|=j,\n\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^{n}j=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j\\ge 2\\) the points of \\(B_j\\) satisfy the linear equation  \n\n\\[\n(j+1)x-(j-1)y=2j,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhich defines a line \\(L_j\\) of slope  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{j+1}{j-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j=1\\) we set \\(L_1\\) to be the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)), also sunny.  \nThus each \\(L_j\\) is sunny and contains exactly the points of \\(B_j\\).  \nSince the slopes \\(\\{m_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are distinct and differ from the slope of \\(L_1\\), the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct.\n\nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(n)}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(S_n\\) and consists entirely of sunny lines.  \nTherefore \\(n\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Interpolation Lemma\n\nWe now show that from any admissible configuration we can increase the number of sunny lines by one.\n\n> **Lemma (Sunny‑line insertion).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k<n\\)).  \n> Then there exists another family \\(\\mathcal L'=\\{L'_1,\\dots ,L'_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny; without loss of generality call it \\(L\\).  \nAll non‑sunny lines have slope in \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nPick a point \\(P\\in S_n\\) that lies **only** on \\(L\\) (such a point exists: otherwise every point of \\(S_n\\) would belong to at least two lines, forcing the total number of distinct lines to be \\(\\le n-1\\), contradicting the hypothesis that the lines are distinct).  \n\nLet \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) be any sunny line passing through \\(P\\) whose slope is different from the slopes of all other lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (this is always possible because the set of forbidden slopes is finite).  \nReplace \\(L\\) by \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\); denote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal L'\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) that were covered by lines other than \\(L\\) remain covered.  \nThe point \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\).  \nAny other point that was covered exclusively by \\(L\\) must lie on the same line as \\(P\\) (otherwise two distinct points would be covered only by \\(L\\), which would force \\(L\\) to be the unique line through each of them, contradicting the distinctness of the lines).  \nHence every such point also lies on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(S_n\\).  \n\nBy construction \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) is sunny and all other lines are unchanged, so the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Induction on the number of sunny lines\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n*Base cases.*  \nThe constructions of §1 give configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a configuration exists for some \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k<n\\).  \nApplying the Lemma we obtain a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \nThus, starting from the known configuration for \\(k=0\\), we can reach every intermediate value \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
