\\(x+y=n\\) we obtain \\((x_h,n-x_h)\\) where \\(x_h=(n-c)/(1+m)\\).\n\n   Because the line is not parallel to any of the three sides, these three intersection points are distinct and lie on the three different sides of the triangle.  \n   Any lattice point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lies on the line must lie strictly between two consecutive side\u2011intersection points; consequently its \\(x\\)-coordinate is an integer strictly between the smallest and the largest of the three \\(x\\)-coordinates above.  \n\n   The smallest possible integer among the three \\(x\\)-coordinates is at least \\(1\\) and the largest is at most \\(n\\). Moreover the two extreme side\u2011intersection points already occupy the two extreme integer abscissae \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to a side). Hence the interior integer abscissae that can appear are among the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), which contains exactly \\(n-2\\) elements. Therefore the line can contain at most one lattice point for each of those abscissae, i.e. at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-2\\) points, and by Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)s+n\\,(n-s)=n^{2}-2s .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered, (1) and (2) give  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-2s\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   s\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Construction that attains every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (no sunny lines).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (4) are non\u2011sunny.  \n   We verify that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n   * If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n     \u2013 When \\(b=1\\) we have \\(a+b=n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\).  \n     \u2013 When \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n   * If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers all required points and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Turning a vertical line into a sunny line.*  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}:\\; y = x-t+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_{t}\\) has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), so it is sunny.  \n   It passes through the two points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,2)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Remove the vertical line \\(x=t\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and insert \\(S_{t}\\).  \n   Denote the new family by \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\).  \n   We must check that no point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) becomes uncovered.\n\n   *Points with \\(x\\neq t\\).*  \n   If a point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a\\neq t\\) then it was already covered by the same line as before (either a remaining vertical line or one of the two diagonals), so it remains covered.\n\n   *Points with \\(x=t\\).*  \n   The points on the removed vertical line are \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-t\\).  \n   \u2013 The point \\((t,1)\\) is now on \\(S_{t}\\).  \n   \u2013 The point \\((t+1,2)\\) (which also belongs to the set when \\(t+1\\le n\\)) is on \\(S_{t}\\).  \n   \u2013 For any remaining \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-t\\) we have \\(t+b\\ge t+2\\).  \n     If \\(t+b=n\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\);  \n     if \\(t+b=n+1\\) it lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n     Both diagonals are still present in \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\).  \n   Thus every point formerly on \\(x=t\\) is still covered.\n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly **one** sunny line.\n\n   *Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.*  \n   Choose an arbitrary subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\), replace each vertical line \\(x=t\\) (\\(t\\in T\\)) by the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) defined in (5).  \n   The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl\\{\\,S_{t}\\mid t\\in T\\bigr\\}\n                \\;\\cup\\;\n                \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus T\\bigr\\}\n                \\;\\cup\\;\n                \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\((n-2)-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines and the two diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   The verification performed for a single replacement shows that after all replacements every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is still covered.  \n   Hence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 together with the counting argument (3) proves that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   The explicit construction (6) shows that each integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) is attainable.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"
