            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the solution so that every claim is rigorously proved.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The set of required points**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n   \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **How many points can a single line contain?**  \n\n   *Lemma 1 (non‑sunny lines).*  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The three families intersect the triangle exactly in its three sides:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\{y=1\\}\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\n   &\\{x=1\\}\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\n   &\\{x+y=n\\}\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,n-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Each of the three sets has exactly \\(n\\) points (the first two share the point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   Any line parallel to one of the three directions is either one of the three sides above or lies completely outside the triangle, hence can meet \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n   *Lemma 2 (sunny lines).*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any side of the triangle, it meets each side in a single point; denote these intersection points by  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{b}=(x_{b},1),\\qquad P_{l}=(1,y_{l}),\\qquad P_{h}=(x_{h},n-x_{h}),\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   x_{b}=\\frac{1-c}{m},\\qquad y_{l}=m+c,\\qquad x_{h}=\\frac{n-c}{1+m}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The three points \\(P_{b},P_{l},P_{h}\\) lie on three different sides of the triangle, hence their \\(x\\)-coordinates satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x_{b}<x_{h}<n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 1\\le x_{h}<x_{b}<n,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   the order depending on the sign of \\(m\\).  \n   Any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\) must have its abscissa \\(a\\) strictly between the smallest and the largest of the three numbers in (2); otherwise the point would belong to a side of the triangle, contradicting the fact that \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to that side.  \n\n   Consequently the possible integer abscissae of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) belong to the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(n-2\\) elements.  \n   For each admissible integer \\(a\\) there is at most one integer \\(b\\) with \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\) (the line is a function of \\(x\\)). Hence \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 2 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-2\\) points, and by Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   Therefore the total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|\\;\\le\\; s\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,n = n^{2}-2s .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-2s\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   2s\\;\\le\\;n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   =\\frac{n(n-2)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n(n-2)}4 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right–hand side of (5) is at most \\(n-2\\); indeed  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-2)}4\\le n-2\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n\\ge 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le n-2 .\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (no sunny lines).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (7) are non‑sunny.  \n   We verify that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n   * If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n     – When \\(b=1\\) we have \\(a+b=n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x+y=n\\).  \n     – When \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n   * If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\); again \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers all required points and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Turning a vertical line into a sunny line.*  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}:\\; y = x-t+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  \n   It passes through the two points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,2)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Remove the vertical line \\(x=t\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and insert \\(S_{t}\\); denote the new family by \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\).  \n   We check that no point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) becomes uncovered.\n\n   *Points with \\(x\\neq t\\).*  \n   Such a point was already covered by a line that is still present, so it remains covered.\n\n   *Points with \\(x=t\\).*  \n   The points on the removed line are \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-t\\).  \n   – \\((t,1)\\) now lies on \\(S_{t}\\).  \n   – \\((t+1,2)\\) (which belongs to the set when \\(t+1\\le n\\)) also lies on \\(S_{t}\\).  \n   – For any remaining \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-t\\) we have \\(t+b\\ge t+2\\).  \n     If \\(t+b=n\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\);  \n     if \\(t+b=n+1\\) it lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n     Both diagonals are still present.  \n   Thus every point formerly on \\(x=t\\) is still covered, and \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.*  \n   Choose an arbitrary subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\), replace each vertical line \\(x=t\\) (\\(t\\in T\\)) by the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) defined in (8).  \n   The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl\\{\\,S_{t}\\mid t\\in T\\bigr\\}\n                \\;\\cup\\;\n                \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus T\\bigr\\}\n                \\;\\cup\\;\n                \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(n-2-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and the two diagonal (non‑sunny) lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   The verification performed for a single replacement shows that after all replacements every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is still covered.  \n\n   Hence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 together with the counting argument (4)–(6) proves that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   The explicit construction (9) shows that each integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) is attainable.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
