            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The set of required points**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n   \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \n   Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **How many points can a single line contain?**  \n\n   *Lemma 1 (non‑sunny lines).*  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) meets\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The three families intersect the triangle exactly in its three sides  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\{y=1\\}\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\n   &\\{x=1\\}\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\n   &\\{x+y=n\\}\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,n-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The first two sets contain \\(n\\) points each (they share \\((1,1)\\));\n   the third set contains \\(n-1\\) points.  \n   Any line parallel to one of the three directions is either one of the\n   sides above or lies completely outside the triangle, hence can meet\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n   *Lemma 2 (sunny lines).*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. a line whose slope is different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any side of the triangle, it meets each\n   side in a single point. Denote the three intersection points by  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{b}=(x_{b},1),\\qquad P_{l}=(1,y_{l}),\\qquad P_{d}=(x_{d},n-x_{d}),\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   x_{b}=\\frac{1-c}{m},\\qquad y_{l}=m+c,\\qquad x_{d}=\\frac{n-c}{1+m}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the three sides are pairwise non‑parallel, the three numbers\n   \\(x_{b},\\,1,\\,x_{d}\\) are distinct; consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   1<\\min\\{x_{b},x_{d}\\}\\le\\max\\{x_{b},x_{d}\\}<n .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\) must have\n   its abscissa \\(a\\) strictly between the smallest and the largest of the\n   numbers in (3); otherwise the point would belong to a side of the triangle,\n   contradicting the fact that \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to that side.\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a set containing exactly \\(n-2\\) integers.  \n   For each admissible integer \\(a\\) there is at most one integer \\(b\\) with\n   \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\) (the line is the graph of a function of \\(x\\)).  \n   Therefore \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 2 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-2\\) points, and by\n   Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|\\;\\le\\; s\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,n = n^{2}-2s .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-2s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   2s\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{n(n-2)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-2)}4\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge3). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (no sunny lines).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (6) are non‑sunny.  \n   We verify that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\))\n     contains \\((a,b)\\).\n   * If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n     – For \\(b=1\\) we have \\(a+b=n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x+y=n\\).  \n     – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n   * If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\); again \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on\n     \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers all required points and contains\n   \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Turning a vertical line into a sunny line.*  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}:\\; y = x-t+1 .\\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  \n   It passes through the two points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,2)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Remove the vertical line \\(x=t\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and insert \\(S_{t}\\);\n   denote the new family by \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\).  \n   We check that no point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) becomes uncovered.\n\n   *Points with \\