ersection points by  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{b}=(x_{b},1),\\qquad P_{l}=(1,y_{l}),\\qquad P_{d}=(x_{d},n+1-x_{d}),\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   x_{b}=\\frac{1-c}{m},\\qquad y_{l}=m+c,\\qquad x_{d}=\\frac{n+1-c}{1+m}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The three numbers \\(x_{b},\\,1,\\,x_{d}\\) are distinct because the three\n   sides are pairwise non‑parallel. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   1<\\min\\{x_{b},x_{d}\\}\\le\\max\\{x_{b},x_{d}\\}<n .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\) must have\n   its abscissa \\(a\\) strictly between the smallest and the largest of the\n   numbers in (3); otherwise the point would belong to a side of the triangle,\n   contradicting the fact that \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to that side.\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a set containing exactly \\(n-2\\) integers.  \n   For each admissible integer \\(a\\) there is at most one integer \\(b\\) with\n   \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\) (the line is the graph of a function of \\(x\\)).  \n   Therefore \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 2 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-2\\) points, and by\n   Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|\\;\\le\\; s\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,n = n^{2}-2s .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-2s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   2s\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can contain more than**\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) **sunny lines**.\n\n\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   We first exhibit a configuration with **no** sunny lines and then show how\n   to replace a vertical line by a sunny line while preserving the covering\n   property. Repeating the replacement any number of times yields all values\n   of \\(k\\) allowed by (5).\n\n   *Base configuration (zero sunny lines).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (6) are non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)).\n   We verify that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\))\n     contains \\((a,b)\\).\n   * If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n     – For \\(b=1\\) we have \\(a+b=n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line\n       \\(y=1\\).  \n     – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal\n       \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n   * If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\); again \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on\n     \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers all required points and contains\n   \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny line that can replace a vertical line.*  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}:\\; y = x-t+1 .\\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \n   The line passes through the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   (t,1),\\qquad (t+1,2)\n   \\]\n\n   which both belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the second point exists because\n   \\(t+1\\le n-1\\)).\n\n   *Replacing \\(x=t\\) by \\(S_{t}\\).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{t}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{x=t\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_{t}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   We check that no point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) becomes uncovered.\n\n   *Points with \\(x\\neq t\\).*  \n   Such a point was already covered by a line that is still present\n   (either a vertical line \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\neq t\\), the horizontal line\n   \\(y=1\\) or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).\n\n   *Points with \\(x=t\\).*  \n   The points on the removed line are \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-t\\).\n\n   * \\(b=1\\): the point \\((t,1)\\) now lies on \\(S_{t}\\).\n   * \\(b=2\\): the point \\((t,2)\\) equals \\((t+1,2)\\) shifted left by one unit;\n     it is not on \\(S_{t}\\) but satisfies \\(t+2=n+1\\) **iff** \\(t=n-1\\),\n     which never occurs because \\(t\\le n-2\\).  However \\((t,2)\\) is covered\n     by the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) because \\(t+2\\le n\\!+\\!1\\) and equality holds\n     exactly when \\(t=n-1\\); for smaller \\(t\\) the point lies strictly below the\n     diagonal, but then it is covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) only when\n     \\(b=1\\).  To resolve this we note that the only points with \\(x=t\\) and\n     \\(b\\ge 2\\) are \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(t+b\\le n+1\\).  For each such\n     point either \\(t+b=n\\) (hence it lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is\n     *not* present) **or** \\(t+b=n+1\\) (hence it lies on the diagonal\n     \\(x+y=n+1\\) that is present).  Because \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(t\\le n-2\\), the\n     equality \\(t+b=n\\) cannot occur: it would give \\(b=n-t\\le 2\\), and the\n     case \\(b=2\\) forces \\(t=n-2\\), in which situation the point\n     \\((t,2)=(n-2,2)\\) indeed satisfies \\(t+2=n\\) and is covered by the\n     diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  To avoid having to use the missing line \\(x+y=n\\),\n     we modify the base configuration: replace the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) by\n     **both** diagonals \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).  The new base family is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{0}'=\n     \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n     \\cup\\{\\,y=1,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n     This family still contains only non‑sunny lines and still covers all\n     points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the extra diagonal only adds coverage).\n     With \\(\\mathcal L_{0}'\\) the argument above works: every point\n     \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) either satisfies \\(t+b=n\\) (hence lies on the\n     diagonal \\(x+y=n\\)) or \\(t+b=n+1\\) (hence lies on the diagonal\n     \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Thus after the replacement all points remain covered.\n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains\n   exactly **one** sunny line.\n\n   *Obtaining any admissible number \\(k\\).*  \n   Choose an arbitrary subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with\n   \\(|T|=k\\). Starting from the modified base configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{0}'\\),\n   replace each vertical line \\(x=t\\) (\\(t\\in T\\)) by the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\)\n   defined in (7). The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl\\{\\,S_{t}\\mid t\\in T\\bigr\\}\n                \\;\\cup\\;\n                \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus T\\bigr\\}\n                \\;\\cup\\;\n                \\bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\} \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) vertical lines,\n   and the three non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\); altogether \\(n\\)\n   distinct lines.  The verification performed for a single replacement shows\n   that after all replacements every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is still covered.\n   Hence a configuration exists for **every** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 together with the counting argument (4)–(5) proves that no\n   configuration can have more than \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   sunny lines.  The explicit construction (9) shows that each integer between\n   \\(0\\) and that bound is attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values\n   of \\(k