 \\(L_a\\) (note that \\(a\\le n\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Hence \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine the following set of \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* **Non‑sunny part** (if any):  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n  \\]\n\n  These are horizontal, thus not sunny.\n\n* **Sunny part** (if any):  \n\n  \\[\n  S_j:\\; y = x-j+1\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of each \\(S_j\\) equals \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \n  Distinctness follows from the different intercepts \\(-j+1\\).\n\nWe claim that the union of these \\(n\\) lines contains every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of the covering property  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b> n-k\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nj:=a-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b>n-k\\) and \\(a\\le k\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le j = a-b+1 \\le k-b+1\\le k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(j\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\).  Substituting \\(j\\) into the equation of \\(S_j\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = x-j+1 \\Longrightarrow b = a-(a-b+1)+1 = b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(S_j\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n\\subseteq\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k} H_i\\Bigr)\\cup\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\,k} S_j\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)).\n\nConversely, Lemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is possible, and Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is possible; the constructions above fill all intermediate values.  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
