 Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and not \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),\\,(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\,(n,1)\\},\n\\qquad |D|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_{0}:x+y=n+1\\) contains exactly the points of \\(D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(D\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nA sunny line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nThe line \\(L_{0}\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \nTwo non\u2011parallel lines intersect in a single point; therefore a sunny line can intersect \\(L_{0}\\) in at most one point, and that intersection belongs to \\(D\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(D\\), and it contains two points of \\(D\\) only if it is horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) or vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)).  \n\n**Proof.**  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal) it meets the diagonal \\(L_{0}\\) in the unique point whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals the line\u2019s constant; similarly a vertical line meets \\(L_{0}\\) in the unique point whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals the line\u2019s constant.  \nIf a line is parallel to \\(L_{0}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) then it either coincides with \\(L_{0}\\) (hence contains all \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D\\)) or is disjoint from \\(D\\).  \nAll other non\u2011sunny lines have slopes \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) and therefore intersect \\(L_{0}\\) in exactly one point. \u220e\n\n\n\nNow let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P\\), and let  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nSplit \\(\\mathcal L\\) into the sunny part \\(\\mathcal L_{s}\\) (size \\(k\\)) and the non\u2011sunny part \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) (size \\(n-k\\)).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each line of \\(\\mathcal L_{s}\\) covers at most one point of \\(D\\); hence the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each line of \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) covers at most two points of \\(D\\).  Consequently the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-k)\\) points of \\(D\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(D\\) must be covered,  \n\n\\[\nk+2(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;|D|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2n-k\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}: x=1 ,\\qquad\n\\ell_{d}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_{v}\\) is vertical, \\(\\ell_{d}\\) has slope \\(-1\\); both are non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_{1},m_{2},\\dots ,m_{k}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y=m_{i}x+b_{i},\n\\qquad\\text{where }b_{i}=i+1-m_{i}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passes through the point \\((1,i+1)\\).  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are distinct and sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  The remaining \\(n-2-k\\) lines (horizontal)  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\