\\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\((a,b)=(n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). ∎\n\nConsequently \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of a sunny line through any uncovered point).\n\nLet \\(P\\in T_n\\).  There exists a line through \\(P\\) whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. a sunny line. Moreover, given finitely many distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_m\\) there exist sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\) such that \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and the \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a fixed point \\(P\\) the set of slopes that would make the line through \\(P\\) parallel to one of the three forbidden directions is the three‑element set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Any other real number yields a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line through \\(P\\) exists.  \n\nGiven distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_m\\), choose for each \\(i\\) a slope \\(s_i\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes already assigned to \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_{i-1}\\).  This is possible because the real line contains infinitely many admissible slopes.  The line with slope \\(s_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) is then sunny and distinct from the previously constructed lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing non‑sunny lines by sunny ones).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\subseteq\\mathcal L_0\\) be any sub‑collection of the lines in (2) with \\(|\\mathcal L|=n-k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  Then there exists a set \\(\\mathcal S\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\\cup\\mathcal S\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line of \\(\\