nto the equation of \\(S_j\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x-j+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b = a-(a-b+1)+1 = b ,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013 the construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the two extreme cases already show that the endpoints \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Extreme families  \n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}H_i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_b\\).  All \\(H_i\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so their slope is \\(0\\) and they are not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nThen the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct, each of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\); this number is never \\(0\\), never infinite, and never \\(-1\\).  Distinct indices give distinct slopes, so the lines are distinct.  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), we may substitute \\(i=a\\) in the equation of \\(L_a\\):\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{a}{a+1}x+\\frac{1}{a+1}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nb=\\frac{a}{a+1}a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a,\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the given \\(b\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=a\\) only when \\(a+b=n+1\\); in any case the equality holds because the right\u2011hand side simplifies to \\(a\\)).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_t:\\;y=t .\n\\]\n\nThese are horizontal, therefore not sunny, and are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y = x-j+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_j\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  The intercepts \\(-j+1\\) are distinct, so the \\(S_j\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the horizontal lines.\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering property\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b>n-k\\).*  \nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nj:=a-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b>n-k\\) and \\(a\\le k\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le j = a-b+1\\le k-b+1\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(j\\) is an integer belonging to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Substituting this value of \\(j\\) into the equation of \\(S_j\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny = x-j+1 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b = a-(a-b+1)+1 = b ,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\nIn either case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n\\subseteq\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{t=1}^{\\,n-k} H_t\\Bigr)\\cup\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\,k} S_j\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConversely, Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, and Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is attainable; the constructions above fill all intermediate values.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

