ubstituting this \\(j\\) into the equation of \\(S_j\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x-(n-k-j+1)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   b = a-(n-k-(n-k-b+a+1)+1)=b ,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013 the construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the two extreme cases already show that the endpoints \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Extreme families  \n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}H_i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_b\\).  All \\(H_i\\) have slope \\(0\\), therefore they are not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nThen the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct, each of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\); this number is never \\(0\\), never infinite, and never \\(-1\\).  Distinct indices give distinct slopes, so the lines are distinct and sunny.  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), we may substitute \\(i=a\\) in the equation of \\(L_a\\):\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{a}{a+1}a+\\frac{1}{a+1}\n   =\\frac{a^{2}+1}{a+1}\n   =\\frac{a(a+1)-a+1}{a+1}\n   =a-\\frac{a-1}{a+1}=a-\\frac{a-1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b=n+1-a\\) when the equality holds and \\(b<a\\) otherwise; in both cases the right\u2011hand side equals the given integer \\(b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_t:\\;y=t .\n\\]\n\nThese are horizontal, therefore not sunny, and are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y = x-(n-k-j+1).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are sunny.  Their intercepts \\(-(n-k-j+1)\\) are distinct, so the \\(S_j\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the horizontal lines.  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering property\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b>n-k\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nj:=n-k-b+a+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b>n-k\\) and \\(a\\le k\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le j = n-k-b+a+1\\le n-k-(n-k+1)+k+1 = k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(j\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  Substituting this value of \\(j\\) into the equation of \\(S_j\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny = x-(n-k-j+1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nb = a-(n-k-(n-k-b+a+1)+1)=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\nIn either case the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines, so  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n\\subseteq\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{t=1}^{\\,n-k} H_t\\Bigr)\\cup\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\,k} S_j\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is attainable, and the construction above fills all intermediate values.  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
