= 2a-(n-k+1),\n\\]\n\nso the equality holds and \\((a,b)\\) indeed lies on \\(S_a\\).  Consequently every point with \\(b>n-k\\) is covered by the sunny line whose index equals its \\(x\\)-coordinate.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe two extreme cases \\(k=0\\) (Lemma 1) and \\(k=n\\) (Lemma 2) show that the endpoints are attainable, and the construction above fills all intermediate values.  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
