 points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that satisfy \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).\n\nAll three cases cover every element of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore the family of lines constructed above indeed covers the whole set.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* By the argument of §1, any admissible family must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited (in §2–§3) a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
