unny lines.**  \n     Take the family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) and add the line \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n     The line \\(D_{n+1}\\) is **not** sunny (its slope is \\(-1\\)).  \n     The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F'_{n+1,k}\\) has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines,\n     covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n  3. **Increasing the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n     Let \\(\\ell\\) be any non‑sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) (so \\(\\ell\\) is vertical,\n     horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n     Remove \\(\\ell\\) from \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\); denote the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines by\n     \\(\\mathcal G\\).  \n     The points of \\(T_{n}\\) that were covered only by \\(\\ell\\) form a set\n     \\(S\\subseteq T_{n}\\) that lies on a single line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n     Because \\(|S|\\le n\\), we can choose **two** distinct points of \\(S\\) and draw the\n     unique line \\(\\sigma\\) through them.  \n     The slope of \\(\\sigma\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\sigma\\) is sunny.\n     Moreover \\(\\sigma\\) contains the two chosen points and therefore covers all of\n     \\(S\\) (any point of \\(S\\) lies on the same line as the two chosen points, because\n     all points of \\(S\\) are collinear).  \n     Consequently the family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal H:=\\mathcal G\\cup\\{\\sigma\\}\n     \\]\n\n     still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\), and now has\n     one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\).\n\n     Adding the outer‑layer line \\(D_{n+1}\\) as in step 2 we obtain a family\n     \\(\\mathcal F'_{n+1,k+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly\n     \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n  4. **Iterating the replacement.**  \n     Starting from a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,0}\\) (which exists by the base construction,\n     see below) we can apply the operation of step 3 repeatedly, each time increasing the\n     number of sunny lines by one, until we reach a family with \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n     Hence for the fixed \\(n\\) we obtain families with every\n     \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n  5. **Induction conclusion.**  \n     The construction of step 2 shows that from a family for \\(n\\) with any admissible\n     \\(k\\) we obtain a family for \\(n+1\\) with the same \\(k\\); step 3 shows that from a\n     family for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) we obtain a family for \\(n+1\\) with \\(k+1\\).\n     Therefore, assuming the statement true for \\(n\\), we obtain it for \\(n+1\\).\n\n* **Base case details (\\(n=3\\)).**  \n  The set  \n\n  \\[\n  T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n  \\]\n\n  must be covered by three distinct lines.\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  k & \\text{family of three lines} \\\\ \\hline\n  0 & \\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\} \\\\[2mm]\n  1 & \\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2x-1\\} \\\\[2mm]\n  2 & \\{x=1,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y= -\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac52\\} \\\\[2mm]\n  3 & \\{y=2x-1,\\;y= -\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac52,\\;y= \\tfrac13 x+ \\tfrac23\\}\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  In each row the three lines are distinct, they cover the three points of\n  \\(T_{3}\\), and the number of sunny lines (i.e. lines whose slope is different from\n  \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) is exactly the indicated \\(k\\).\n\nSince the base case holds and the inductive step is valid, the statement is true for\nall integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a set of }n\\text{ distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
