,b)\\in S_n\\).  By definition \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_x\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_y\\).  \n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\((a,b)\\) lies on or below the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  In particular, when \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point is on \\(L_d\\); when \\(a+b<n+1\\) it is still inside the triangle bounded by \\(L_x, L_y, L_d\\) and therefore already covered by the union of those three lines.  Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of \\(L_x, L_y, L_d\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Construction for any \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \nStarting from the three non\u2011sunny lines of Lemma\u202f1, add the following \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; therefore every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  The lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n*Coverage is preserved.*  \nThe line \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\).  Since \\(i\\le k\\le n-3\\),\n\n\\[\n2i \\le 2k \\le 2(n-3) \\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((i,i)\\in S_n\\).  Moreover \\((i,i)\\) already lies on the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_d\\) when \\(2i=n+1\\); if \\(2i<n+1\\) the point is still covered by the union of \\(L_x, L_y, L_d\\).  Adding the line \\(S_i\\) therefore does not remove any previously covered point, and it does not create any uncovered point.  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = \\{L_x, L_y, L_d\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all of \\(S_n\\).  The total number of lines is \\(3+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(S_i\\)\u2019s) are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (At least