llel to any forbidden direction).  The \\(i\\)-th sunny line is  \n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n   Each \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\).  Because \\(i\\le k\\le n-3\\) we have \\(2i\\le 2(n-3)\\le n+1\\); hence \\((i,i)\\in S_n\\).  The point \\((i,i)\\) is already covered by the non\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) (it lies on a vertical line \\(x=i\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=i\\) that we have included), so the addition of the sunny lines does not destroy the covering property.  \n\n   The resulting family  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L = \\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Combining the two lemmas.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-3\\); Lemma\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) can indeed be realised.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v_1=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf v_2=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf v_3=(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (A necessary bound)\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, then at least three lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny; consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(1+1\\le n+1\\) and \\(1+2\\le n+1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nAny line that contains two of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) has direction vector equal to \\(\\mathbf v_1\\), \\(\\mathbf v_2\\) or \\(\\mathbf v_3\\); all three directions are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  To cover the three points we therefore need at least three distinct lines that are not sunny.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Covering by non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(t\\ge 3\\) be an integer.  There exist \\(t\\) distinct lines, each parallel to one of \\(\\mathbf v_1,\\mathbf v_2,\\mathbf v_3\\), that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose integers  \n\n\\[\nv\\ge 1,\\;h\\ge 1,\\;d\\ge 1\\qquad\\text{with}\\qquad v+h+d=t .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,v\\,\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,h\\,\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=n+1-\\ell\\mid \\ell=0,1,\\dots ,d-1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\) are vertical (parallel to \\(\\mathbf v_2\\)), those in \\(\\mathcal H\\) are horizontal (parallel to \\(\\mathbf v_1\\)), and those in \\(\\mathcal D\\) have slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(\\mathbf v_3\\)).  The total number of lines is \\(|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal D|=v+h+d=t\\).\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Three cases are possible.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le v\\).  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>v\\) but \\(b\\le h\\).  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).  Because \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nn+1-(a+b)\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,d-1\\},\n\\]\n\nso the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1-(a+b)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\