ies of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,v\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,h\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=n+1-\\ell\\mid \\ell=0,1,\\dots ,d-1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\) are vertical, those in \\(\\mathcal H\\) are horizontal, and those in \\(\\mathcal D\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore every line in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) is non‑sunny.  The total number of lines is \\(v+h+d=t\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have three mutually exclusive possibilities.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le v\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>v\\) but \\(b\\le h\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn+1-(a+b)\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,d-1\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause otherwise \\(a+b\\le n-d\\) would imply \\(b\\le h\\), contradicting the hypothesis of the case.  Hence the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1-(a+b)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  Setting \\(\\mathcal N_t:=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) yields the required covering family of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Realising any admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  Then there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPut \\(t=n-k\\); by the hypothesis on \\(k\\) we have \\(t\\ge 3\\).  \nApply Lemma 2 to obtain a covering family \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nNow introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines, all of slope \\(1\\):\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\).  Because \\(i\\le k\\le n-3\\),\n\n\\[\n2i\\le 2(n-3)\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((i,i)\\in S_n\\).  Moreover \\((i,i)\\) already lies on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (if \\(i\\le v\\)) or on the horizontal line \\(y=i\\) (if \\(i\\le h\\)) belonging to \\(\\mathcal N_t\\); consequently the addition of the sunny lines does not affect the covering property.\n\nThe final collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Result  \n\nLemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
