            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Upper bound)\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\), then at least three of the lines are non‑sunny. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(S_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nAny line that contains two of these points is respectively vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three directions are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  To cover the three points we therefore need at least three distinct lines, and each of those three lines must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Covering by non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(t\\ge 3\\).  Choose integers \\(v\\ge 1,\\;h\\ge 1\\) with \\(v+h=t\\) and define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,v\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,h\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen the family \\(\\mathcal N_t:=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\) consists of \\(t\\) distinct lines, each parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis (hence non‑sunny), and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Three mutually exclusive cases are possible.\n\n1. **Case \\(a\\le v\\).**  \n   The point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n2. **Case \\(a>v\\) and \\(b\\le h\\).**  \n   The point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n\n3. **Case \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).**  \n   Then \\(a\\ge v+1\\) and \\(b\\ge h+1\\), so  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\ge v+h+2=t+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we also have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   t+2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   In the applications below we will always have \\(t=n-k\\) with \\(k\\ge 0\\); hence \\(t\\le n\\) and the inequality \\(t\\le n-1\\) is satisfied whenever \\(k\\ge 1\\).  If \\(k=0\\) then \\(t=n\\) and the third case cannot occur because it would require \\(a+b\\ge n+2\\), contradicting \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Consequently the third case never actually arises for the values of \\(t\\) we shall use, and every point of \\(S_n\\) is already covered by the first two cases.\n\nThus every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Existence for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  Then there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet \\(t=n-k\\) (so \\(t\\ge 3\\)).  Apply Lemma 2 with the concrete choice  \n\n\\[\nv=n-k-1,\\qquad h=1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\le n-3\\), we have \\(v\\ge 2\\) and \\(h=1\\) is admissible; the resulting family \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) consists of \\(t\\) vertical/horizontal (non‑sunny) lines and covers \\(S_n\\).\n\nNow introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines, all of slope \\(1\\):\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\).  Because \\(i\\le k\\le n-3\\),\n\n\\[\n2i\\le 2(n-3)\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((i,i)\\in S_n\\).  Hence the sunny lines also cover points of \\(S_n\\); they do not interfere with the covering already provided by \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).  The sunny lines are distinct from one another (different intercepts) and from the vertical/horizontal lines (different slopes), so the total number of distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N_t|+k = (n-k)+k = n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Result  \n\nLemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
