nt of \\(S_n\\), then at least three of the lines are non\u2011sunny. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(S_n\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nAny line that contains two of these points is respectively vertical (\\(x=1\\)), horizontal (\\(y=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  All three directions are forbidden for a sunny line, so a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).  Hence three distinct lines are needed to cover the three points, and each of those three lines is non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Covering by vertical and horizontal lines)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=n-k-1,\\qquad h=k+1,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,v\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,h\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le v\\).  \nThen the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>v\\).  \nSince \\(v=n-k-1\\), we have \\(a\\ge v+1=n-k\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1=h .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,h\\}\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn either case the point belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Introducing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with