ence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\cup\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   All are vertical or horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have either \\(a\\le n-2\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\); in those latter cases \\(b\\le 2\\) and the point lies on one of the two horizontal lines.  \n   Thus every required lattice point is covered, and exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines appear.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that any configuration with a sunny line leads to a contradiction, so the only feasible value is \\(k=0\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f3 exhibits a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that satisfies the covering requirement.  \n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Three non\u2011sunny lines are necessary)\n\nThe points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\; P_2=(1,2),\\; P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(S_n\\).  \nAny line containing two of them is respectively vertical (\\(x=1\\)), horizontal (\\(y=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\)).  \nAll three directions are forbidden for a sunny line, therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).  \nConsequently at least three distinct lines are required to cover the three points, and each of those three lines is non\u2011sunny.  \nHence for any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (A sunny line forces a second sunny line)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) contains at least one sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(P_1=(1,1)\\) (otherwise rename the axes).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nConsider the two remaining special points \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\).  \nThey cannot lie on \\(\\ell\\); therefore they must be covered by two other lines, say \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_3\\).  \nIf both \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_3\\) were non\u2011sunny, then each would be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA simple inspection of the four possibilities shows that at least one of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) would remain uncovered:\n\n*If \\(\\ell_2\\) is vertical (\\(x=1\\)), then \\(\\ell_3\\) must be horizontal (\\(y=2\\)) or slope \\(-1\\).  \nIn either case the point \\((n,1)\\) (which belongs to \\(S_n\\)) is not on \\(\\ell\\) (its \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(1\\neq m n+ c\\)) nor on \\(\\ell_2\\) (its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(n\\neq1\\)) nor on \\(\\ell_3\\) (its \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(1\\neq2\\) and it does not satisfy the equation of a slope \\(-1\\) line through \\((1,2)\\)).  \n\nThe other three configurations are analogous and each leaves uncovered a point of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\nThus at least one of \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) must also be sunny.  \nHence any admissible family containing a sunny line necessarily contains **at least two** sunny lines.\n\nRepeating the same argument with the new sunny line shows that the existence of a sunny line forces the existence of a third sunny line, and so on.  \nConsequently the