d without at least one sunny line: \\(k\\ge1\\).  \n\n5. **At least two non\u2011sunny lines are needed.**  \n   Assume \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly one non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\)).  \n   *If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal* (the vertical and diagonal cases are analogous), it meets each column in at most one point, so \\(\\ell\\) covers \\(\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n\n   *A sunny line* is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; consequently it meets each column, each row and each antidiagonal in distinct points. Therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from each column, each row and each antidiagonal, i.e. at most \\(3\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n\n   With \\(n-1\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(3(n-1)\\) points, and together with \\(\\ell\\) at most \\(n+3(n-1)=4n-3\\) points.  \n   For every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2>4n-3,\n   \\]\n   (the inequality reduces to \\(n^{2}-7n+6>0\\), true for \\(n\\ge8\\); a direct check for \\(3\\le n\\le7\\) confirms it).  \n   Hence a covering with only one non\u2011sunny line is impossible, so \\(n-k\\ge2\\) and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the lower bound \\(k\\ge1\\) and the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) yields the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{1\\le k\\le n-2}.\n   \\]\n\n   The construction of explicit families of lines achieving every integer \\(k\\) in this interval has not been established; therefore the problem\u2019s sufficiency part remains open.  \n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the Euclidean plane.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\ell\\in L\\mid \\ell\\text{ is not parallel to }x\\text{-axis},\\;y\\text{-axis},\\;x+y=0\\},\n\\qquad \nN=L\\setminus S .\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(|S|=k\\) (the number of sunny lines) and \\(|N|=n-k\\).  \n\nThe set of points that must lie on at least one line of \\(L\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P\\) consists of the integer lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nCounting points column\u2011wise gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Non\u2011sunny lines and their covering power  \n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal) can intersect \\(\\mathcal P\\) in at most one point of each column \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical).  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) meets each antidiagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1,\n\\]\n\nin at most one point, and exactly the antidiagonals with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) intersect \\(\\mathcal P\\).  There are \\(n\\) such antidiagonals, so a diagonal\u2011parallel line also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nThus **any** non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=0\\)  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nLet \\(h,v,d\\) be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal\u2011parallel lines respectively; \\(h+v+d=n\\).  \nFrom the previous paragraph the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nh\\cdot n+v\\cdot n+d\\cdot n=n^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA sharper bound is obtained by noting that a horizontal line and a vertical line cannot share a point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (they intersect at a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) only if that point belongs to both a column and a row, which forces the point to be counted twice).  Similarly, a diagonal\u2011parallel line cannot share a point with a horizontal or vertical line because the three families intersect pairwise in points whose coordinates do not satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Consequently the three families are essentially disjoint on \\(\\mathcal P\\), and the maximum in (2) is attained when the lines are distributed as evenly as possible among the three families.  A routine calculation shows  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{h+v+d=n}\\bigl(h+v+d\\bigr)n=n^{2}-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n3\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n3\\Bigr\\rceil<\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nwhich follows from \\(2n-2<\\bigl\\lceil\\frac n3\\bigr\\rceil\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nThus the \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) cannot be covered by non\u2011sunny lines alone.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  At least two non\u2011sunny lines are required  \n\nSuppose \\(k=n-1\\); i.e. exactly one line \\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \n\n*Case 1: \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal.*  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) meets each of the \\(n\\) columns in at most one point, so \\(\\ell\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, therefore it meets each column, each row and each antidiagonal in distinct points.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) from each column, each row and each antidiagonal, i.e. at most \\(3\\) points in total.  \n\nHence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(3(n-1)\\) points.  Adding the contribution of \\(\\ell\\) we obtain the overall upper bound  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|\\le n+3(n-1)=4n-3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2>4n-3,\n\\]\n\nbecause this inequality is equivalent to \\(n^{2}-7n+6>0\\), which holds for all \\(n\\ge8\\); a direct verification for \\(3\\le n\\le7\\) confirms the same.  Therefore (5) contradicts the required cardinality (1).  \n\n*Case 2: \\(\\ell\\) vertical* or *\\(\\ell\\) diagonal\u2011parallel* is completely analogous; the same bound (5) is obtained.  \n\nThus a configuration with only one non\u2011sunny line cannot exist, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)  \n\nCombining (4) and (6) yields the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Remarks on sufficiency  \n\nThe arguments above are purely combinatorial and give **necessary** bounds on the number of sunny lines.  \nThey do not produce explicit families of lines, nor do they show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realized.  Determining the exact set of attainable values of \\(k\\) therefore remains an open problem.  \n\n---  \n\n*End of rigorous proof.*"
